1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,550
 So this is Blender. It can do 3D modeling, texturing, lighting, shading, and rendering. And I'm going to show you how

2
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:12,620
 to do all of those things whilst you make your very own donut.

3
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:19,560
 And I know this looks complex and challenging, but I promise you it is achievable for even a complete beginner that has

4
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:22,200
 never opened Blender, such as yourself.

5
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,920
 And I know this because millions of beginners have already made their donut because this is the fifth version of this

6
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:28,760
 tutorial.

7
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:32,800
 I remake it for every major Blender release, this one for Blender 5.0.

8
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:39,320
 And speaking of which, if you have already made a donut from a previous version, rather than make another one, I've

9
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:43,660
 actually just released a course designed for post-donut beginners.

10
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:46,100
 So if you're interested in that, you can click the link below.

11
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,020
 For everybody else, you need to download Blender.

12
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:51,320
 So if you haven't already, click the link below.

13
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:55,500
 That'll take you to Blender.org and then just hit the big download button.

14
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:59,400
 Now, if you're on Windows, that'll download an executable, which you should install.

15
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,860
 If you're on a Mac, it's an application.

16
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:03,460
 You drag to your applications folder.

17
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:10,180
 And also, if you're at school or you're on a work computer that won't let you install anything, you can still use Blender.

18
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:15,020
 If you download the portable zip version of Blender, then just unzip it.

19
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,120
 You can run Blender from the folder.

20
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:18,040
 Pretty cool.

21
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:22,460
 Anyway, however you got Blender, open it and you should see this.

22
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:27,080
 Then just click anywhere off the splash screen to close it and you're in Blender.

23
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,100
 Hey, welcome.

24
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:33,900
 So this big section, this big gray area here, this is your 3D viewport.

25
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:40,680
 And it's where you'll spend probably 98% of your time because it's the 3D world.

26
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:45,200
 Whether you're making a character, you're building an environment, it all happens right here.

27
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:49,480
 So the first thing you need to learn is how to move around in this space.

28
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:53,520
 And you can orbit by middle mouse pushing.

29
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:57,920
 So your scroll wheel, if you just push it in until it clicks, that's your middle mouse button.

30
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:00,640
 And then if you just drag that around, you can orbit.

31
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,320
 If you don't have a middle mouse button, a lot of people don't.

32
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:05,900
 They're on like a laptop with a trackpad or something.

33
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:07,160
 You guys will struggle.

34
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,400
 It's definitely easier if you have a mouse.

35
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:15,340
 But in a pinch, top right-hand corner, you just mouse over this little circle and then just click and drag on that.

36
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:16,840
 That'll do the same thing.

37
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:24,560
 Or the third alternative way to do it, edit, preferences, and then underneath input, emulate three-button mouse.

38
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:29,280
 And then if you hold down alt and then left-click drag, that will do the same thing.

39
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:30,320
 I don't want that.

40
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:34,300
 By the way, whilst I am here, a value that you will want to change.

41
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:40,270
 If you're on a 4K monitor, you will probably have noticed that, like, Blender looks like this, where, like, everything

42
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:42,580
 is really super tiny and you have to squint to see it.

43
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:49,740
 So underneath interface, I change my resolution scale to at least a 1.5, usually like 1.8.

44
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:53,180
 But for tutorials, I go even higher so you can really see everything.

45
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,860
 But it's obviously much easier to use when you can see things clearly.

46
00:02:56,860 --> 00:03:00,100
 Okay, so middle mouse drag, that's how you orbit around.

47
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,360
 You can see we're focusing on our cube at the moment, though.

48
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:05,340
 So how do we move to somewhere else?

49
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:11,200
 You do that by shift middle mouse drag, and that will pan you somewhere else.

50
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:16,680
 You can also, over here on the side there, click that little hand, that will do the same thing.

51
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:21,140
 But yeah, shift middle mouse drag or middle mouse drag to orbit.

52
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:22,320
 And what about zoom?

53
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:27,730
 You can probably guess you might have already done it by mistake, but just zooming by scrolling in and out on your

54
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:28,380
 wheel.

55
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:32,720
 Or, again, you can go to the little magnifying glass there, just click on that and do that.

56
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:35,800
 Or, alternatively, control middle mouse.

57
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,320
 And then that will also move you in as well.

58
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:40,860
 So I've thrown a lot of keyboard shortcuts at you already.

59
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:45,020
 Middle mouse for this, shift middle mouse to pan, scroll.

60
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:49,060
 If you don't want to have to remember all the hotkeys, because I'm going to throw a lot more at you.

61
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:52,840
 If you don't want to have to write them down yourself, I have done it for you.

62
00:03:52,880 --> 00:04:00,260
 So I put together a Google Doc with all of the most common hotkeys and shortcuts that you might need to use.

63
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:01,380
 And it's free.

64
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:04,220
 So just click the link below and you can print it out.

65
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:05,280
 You can stick it on your wall.

66
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:06,420
 A lot of people do that.

67
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:07,960
 And it's a big time saver.

68
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:08,800
 Okay.

69
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:10,800
 Now, it's really common to, like, get lost.

70
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,680
 Like, one way is to, like, end up upside down like this.

71
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,300
 And then you're like, which way is up?

72
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,260
 How do I, you know?

73
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:26,560
 If you just over here, these little, this gizmo thing, these colored circle things with lines, those are your axes.

74
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:29,540
 So if you just click on one of those, that'll reorient yourself.

75
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,820
 So the Z, that's the top axes.

76
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:33,780
 And then you just, you're back, right?

77
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:36,720
 You know which way is up because it's, it's, it's that way, right?

78
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:37,660
 Which way that one is pointing.

79
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:41,340
 Another way, like, if you got, like, really off into the weeds, like, you were over here.

80
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:44,800
 And then you're like, how do I, how do I get back to where I was?

81
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:50,100
 If you've got an object selected and you just want to focus on, like, just fly to it.

82
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,560
 And this is a really common hotkey.

83
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,580
 You use it all the time.

84
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:56,800
 It's numpad period.

85
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:58,640
 And that'll just fly you to that.

86
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:04,180
 If you don't have a numpad, like, you know, a lot of keyboards today, they don't sell them with numpads anymore, I

87
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,380
 guess, because people aren't accountants.

88
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:11,380
 They don't need to quickly type in numbers, but I love them because it's really useful for Blender.

89
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:19,440
 If you don't have one, you can also use the tilde key, which is that little key underneath your escape key.

90
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:25,700
 So if you hold that down and then just drag over where it says view selected and then release, that'll do the exact

91
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:26,580
 same thing.

92
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:31,940
 So that is how you can quickly change focus to the selected object.

93
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:35,320
 And, yeah, as I said, you do this all the time.

94
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:39,040
 But, yeah, I'm going to be using numpad period from now on.

95
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:39,820
 Okay.

96
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:41,320
 So we're making a donut.

97
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,300
 We do not need our cube here.

98
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:44,180
 So let's delete it.

99
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:46,220
 You can just hit delete on your keyboard.

100
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:50,160
 Or what I like to do is hit X, which is the alternative way.

101
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:53,560
 And it's just easy because my finger is, like, right there.

102
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:58,270
 I know it's another hotkey to remember, but you delete stuff a lot as well, and it's helpful to not have to find that

103
00:05:58,270 --> 00:05:58,560
 key.

104
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,500
 So X and then delete.

105
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:06,200
 And now I want to add a new object, and I can do that by going to add at the top of the screen there.

106
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:12,220
 Or alternatively, the hotkey, another one to throw at you, shift A, and that'll just put it wherever your cursor is,

107
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:13,760
 and it's the exact same list.

108
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:17,340
 But this list here is, I mean, it's extensive.

109
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:21,200
 There's stuff in here that I have never used in my 21 years of using Blender.

110
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:22,100
 Speaker.

111
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:23,940
 Never, ever used it.

112
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:25,100
 Don't know what it's for.

113
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:28,640
 Most of the time, what you're looking for is underneath mesh.

114
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:31,640
 So these are your primitive mesh objects.

115
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:38,210
 And generally, when you're making something, you pick one of these that most resembles or is closest to what it is you're

116
00:06:38,210 --> 00:06:38,880
 making.

117
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,240
 Like, if you're making a mailbox, you might start with a cube.

118
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,500
 You're making a lamppost.

119
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:44,660
 You might start with a cylinder.

120
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:47,960
 We're starting, well, we're trying to make a donut.

121
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:52,700
 So we need to start with a torus, which is basically a donut.

122
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:53,280
 Look at that.

123
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:54,540
 So that's cool.

124
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:57,520
 It's a wimpy-looking donut, though, right?

125
00:06:57,520 --> 00:06:59,580
 It's very, very flimsy.

126
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:02,480
 We want to make it look more thicker, right?

127
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:09,400
 So in the bottom left-hand corner, there's a little box that says add torus, and this has additional options for us.

128
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:14,940
 Now, if you accidentally clicked off after you added the torus, that box is gone.

129
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:20,740
 You can momentarily bring it back by hitting F9, but that's only if you haven't changed something.

130
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:25,740
 So essentially, yeah, it's called the last used function in Blender.

131
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:31,860
 So immediately after you do an action in Blender, sometimes you see in the bottom left-hand corner additional options,

132
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:34,040
 which you can tweak and change things if you need.

133
00:07:34,340 --> 00:07:37,540
 So if you don't see it, it just means you've done something else and it's gone.

134
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:41,360
 But yeah, you can bring it back momentarily by hitting F9 sometimes.

135
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:43,880
 But I usually just, like, redo it.

136
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:47,960
 Anyways, so first thing I want to change is the size of this thing.

137
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:51,040
 So it is one meter, which is like three feet for the Americans.

138
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:52,200
 It's a big donut.

139
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:53,700
 Probably shouldn't be that big.

140
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:55,740
 It should be about 10% of that.

141
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:59,080
 So 0.1, which is 10 centimeters, roughly about that.

142
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:07,520
 And then if I zoom in by scrolling forward, I just need to change my minor radius until it looks somewhat like a donut.

143
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:11,820
 So I want to just orbit up, right, and around.

144
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,620
 So I'm just middle mouse moving so I can see the inside.

145
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:22,700
 Then I'll just scroll in like so until, yeah, and I'm just going to change this value until it looks like a donut.

146
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:27,440
 Now, you can see as I move this, like, it's going really quick and I can't get it on the right value.

147
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:34,360
 So when you're clicking and dragging on a value in Blender, if you hold down shift, that will use smaller increments.

148
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:41,070
 And that's another one you use all the time because it's, yeah, sometimes you need small values and it's helpful to get

149
00:08:41,070 --> 00:08:43,000
 smaller increments like that.

150
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:44,640
 So that's pretty good.

151
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:45,980
 I might go with that.

152
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:49,400
 Yeah, about a 0.057.

153
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:50,300
 Okay.

154
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:55,900
 And then the other thing you can change here is like the resolution of the donut.

155
00:08:55,900 --> 00:09:00,260
 You know, you can go like really PlayStation 1 graphics, maybe.

156
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:09,900
 Well, I'm going to keep it at 48, but I'm going to increase the other one to about, yeah, let's go 18 like that.

157
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:13,340
 16, 18.

158
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:14,740
 Yeah, we'll go 18.

159
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:19,720
 And that's just a little bit more resolution and it's just going to help us a little bit later on when we start to

160
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:20,700
 shape the donut.

161
00:09:20,700 --> 00:09:21,680
 Okay.

162
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:25,340
 But once I've got that, I can just click off and now those options are gone.

163
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:26,000
 We've committed.

164
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:26,840
 That's it.

165
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:29,600
 That is the starting point of our donut.

166
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:35,260
 Now, the next thing you're probably wondering is it still kind of looks like PlayStation 1 graphics, Andrew.

167
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:39,220
 And that's because it has flat shading.

168
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:41,360
 So basically there's two states for a mesh.

169
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:45,380
 There's smooth shading or there is flat shading and it defaults to flat.

170
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:50,060
 If you want to change it to smooth, you just right click the object and then go shade smooth.

171
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:50,880
 Like that.

172
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:51,740
 And there we go.

173
00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:52,260
 It's fixed.

174
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:54,800
 So basically shade smooth without getting too technical.

175
00:09:54,800 --> 00:10:00,180
 Anytime there is like two faces, it just kind of smooths out that point like fakery.

176
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:07,640
 So it's not actually adding any additional computation times, which is important, but it's appearing to the eye to be

177
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:08,480
 smooth.

178
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:08,940
 Okay.

179
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:15,200
 So in previous donut versions, we would start making the icing.

180
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:19,860
 And although that's fun to make like dribbling icing, it can be a little complicated.

181
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:25,000
 A lot of users ran into their first roadblocks at that time.

182
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,440
 So instead, we're going to do something a little bit easier to start with and more practical, and that's to make a

183
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:31,320
 coffee mug.

184
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:38,400
 So we need to add in a new object, which if you can remember from before, it's the add menu at the top there or the hot

185
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,660
 key, as I like to use, shift A.

186
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:45,140
 And then underneath mesh, you're looking for something that most resembles the object you're trying to create.

187
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:46,780
 No prizes for guessing.

188
00:10:46,780 --> 00:10:47,880
 The cylinder.

189
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:49,060
 Okay.

190
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:50,780
 So it comes in very, very big.

191
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:53,080
 It's completely engulfing our donut.

192
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:57,200
 So again, in the bottom left-hand corner, click that little add cylinder.

193
00:10:57,200 --> 00:11:00,780
 And then I'm going to change the radius, first of all, just to bring this back.

194
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:06,580
 You can like do this after the fact, like resize something, but it just helps to, you know, at this point, see it all

195
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:07,380
 together.

196
00:11:07,380 --> 00:11:13,100
 I think I'm going to make it around holding down shift and just kind of drag it back and forth.

197
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:16,500
 Yeah, about 0.13 or exactly 0.13.

198
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:19,240
 And then the depth, this is just the height of it.

199
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:23,420
 And again, you can change this later on, but just want to see it roughly the right size.

200
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:25,820
 The big one that's important is the vertices.

201
00:11:25,820 --> 00:11:29,800
 And we're actually going to make this look more low res.

202
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:32,440
 Now, why would we make it look more low res?

203
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:35,020
 Because now it's obviously very jagged.

204
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:41,530
 The reason for this is that very soon we're going to be adding in something called a subsurf modifier, which adds over

205
00:11:41,530 --> 00:11:45,200
 the top of our existing mesh, it adds more geometry.

206
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:47,720
 Like it doubles the geometry and it kind of smooths it out.

207
00:11:47,720 --> 00:11:54,400
 So actually, if you start too high like this, then you're adding like doubling that geometry and doubling it again.

208
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:58,160
 And then you can quickly end up with something that's too computationally heavy.

209
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:04,850
 So when you're working and you know you're going to be doing something sub div, sometimes it helps to start like chunkier,

210
00:12:04,850 --> 00:12:05,580
 low res.

211
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:11,260
 Anyway, just trust me and set it to 14 like that.

212
00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:13,220
 Okay, great.

213
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:16,280
 So we've got another object and they are completely overlapping.

214
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:18,440
 So how do we separate things?

215
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:20,380
 Some of you might already know this.

216
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:21,540
 You can guess, right?

217
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:25,440
 Left-hand side there, there's a tool that looks like it would be able to move something.

218
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:26,880
 It's the move tool.

219
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,160
 So this is one way to move things.

220
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:31,500
 You activate that and you get this little gizmo.

221
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:34,040
 And look, I can move it and I can separate them.

222
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:36,780
 So this is moving things along the axes.

223
00:12:36,780 --> 00:12:42,960
 You can also just drag on that, click and drag on that little white circle there.

224
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:45,340
 And then that's like freehand drag around.

225
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:53,360
 I will say most Blender users don't actually use and move things this way because it's a lot of effort every time you

226
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:58,500
 want to move something to like go to the sidebar, activate the tool, you know, click it, move it.

227
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,200
 It's like most people don't do that.

228
00:13:00,200 --> 00:13:03,480
 Instead, they remember the hot key and the hot key to move in Blender.

229
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:04,940
 And you'll be using this a lot.

230
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:07,120
 So again, this is on my keyboard shortcut.

231
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:12,400
 It's all in there, but the hot key is G, G for grab.

232
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:20,500
 Now, you will notice very quickly that when you tap G, the moment you tap it, you are already in the move state.

233
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:24,300
 So it's now attached to my cursor, even though I didn't click.

234
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:24,640
 Okay.

235
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:25,660
 So, and here's the other thing.

236
00:13:25,660 --> 00:13:28,820
 My cursor could be on the other side of the screen.

237
00:13:28,820 --> 00:13:30,760
 It doesn't have to be anywhere near the object.

238
00:13:30,940 --> 00:13:37,450
 And then the moment you tap G, it's now anchored to that cursor and even more, I think it's cool when you go to the

239
00:13:37,450 --> 00:13:42,400
 other side of the screen, it actually, your cursor appears on the other side.

240
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:45,420
 So it's just like an infinitely repeating thing there.

241
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:51,210
 But at first, this is like hard to wrap your head around because Blender does this for a lot of things, like not just

242
00:13:51,210 --> 00:13:54,060
 moving something, but also rotating something.

243
00:13:54,220 --> 00:14:00,400
 The moment I tap R, which is how to rotate something, or S, okay, that's, I thought I might as well just teach you all

244
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:01,920
 three at the same time.

245
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:07,280
 So G to move something, G for grab, R to rotate, or S to scale something.

246
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:13,660
 But you'll see in each of those states, the moment you hit the key, no matter where your cursor is, it's in that state.

247
00:14:13,660 --> 00:14:19,020
 And that's actually a good thing because, as I said, the number of times you need to move things and rotate and scale

248
00:14:19,020 --> 00:14:19,860
 things in 3D,

249
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:27,810
 if you had to, every single time, change the tool, then move your cursor over to it, click it, activate it, that's a

250
00:14:27,810 --> 00:14:29,240
 lot of extra steps.

251
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:34,500
 So it's actually really cool to have this, like, just one tap key and it's already activated.

252
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:44,790
 Anyways, okay, so, but whilst I'm in the move state by tapping G, I can also lock it to a specific axis, just like with

253
00:14:44,790 --> 00:14:46,660
 those arrows that you saw before.

254
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:50,920
 So just when it's in that move state, you tap the axes you want to move it on.

255
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:58,660
 So that's the X axes, the Y brings it back and forwards, and the Z is up and down, okay?

256
00:14:58,660 --> 00:14:59,420
 And that's correct.

257
00:14:59,420 --> 00:15:02,500
 Blender is Z up, Y backwards and forwards.

258
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:04,560
 Different 3D applications have different things.

259
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:07,620
 There's no, like, standard, but, yeah, that's how Blenders is.

260
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:13,270
 And by the way, if you forget which letter is which axes, you can actually see them up there in that little circle

261
00:15:13,270 --> 00:15:13,700
 there.

262
00:15:13,700 --> 00:15:16,900
 So, again, X, Y, and Z.

263
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:21,680
 Anyways, I want to move this actually just a little bit further back right there.

264
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:27,180
 So I want my donut to be the front and center, and I want this to be at the back there.

265
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:31,020
 But then how do I change my view so I'm no longer just pivoting on my donut?

266
00:15:31,020 --> 00:15:34,020
 I want to shift my focus to that selected object.

267
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:35,900
 It's number pad, period.

268
00:15:36,360 --> 00:15:42,580
 Or, again, holding down that tilde key under your escape button and then hitting view selected is the same thing.

269
00:15:42,580 --> 00:15:47,120
 Okay, so let's make this look like a coffee mug.

270
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:49,680
 So we have to edit this object.

271
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:53,960
 And to edit an object, we have to change what mode we are in.

272
00:15:53,960 --> 00:16:00,470
 So in the top left-hand corner of the screen, there's a little drop-down, which if you click, we can change it into

273
00:16:00,470 --> 00:16:01,340
 edit mode.

274
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:04,940
 Okay, so when you're in edit mode, you can see the state has changed.

275
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:08,960
 The object that was selected now has a bunch of points and lines on it.

276
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:15,700
 And you'll also notice whilst you're in edit mode, you can't actually select any object.

277
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:19,600
 And it's worth pointing out this because this is a really common thing.

278
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:24,220
 A lot of beginners, they say, I don't know what happened, but I couldn't select anything in Blender.

279
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:27,320
 And usually, it's just they're in the wrong state.

280
00:16:27,320 --> 00:16:28,440
 They're in edit mode.

281
00:16:28,440 --> 00:16:29,060
 They forgot.

282
00:16:29,060 --> 00:16:32,560
 And then they try to, like, come over here and then click something.

283
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:34,400
 And they're like, why is it not selecting?

284
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:38,860
 Okay, so you have to be in object mode if you want to select an object.

285
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:41,920
 And if you want to edit something, you have to be in edit mode.

286
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:47,980
 But going to this drop-down and doing this time and again would, again, be annoying.

287
00:16:48,420 --> 00:16:52,260
 So Blender, of course, has a hotkey for it, and that is Tab.

288
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:58,920
 So Tab will just cycle you in and out of object and edit mode, just those two states,

289
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:01,740
 which is great because you need to use this all the time.

290
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:06,860
 So you usually come in, do some editing, go back into object mode, select something else,

291
00:17:06,860 --> 00:17:09,280
 edit that one, go back to this.

292
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:12,440
 So that's a good one to remember, Tab.

293
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:16,180
 All right, so in edit mode, when I'm in this state here,

294
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:19,480
 these little points here, these are called vertices.

295
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:24,400
 These are the points that make up the, yeah, the shape of the mesh, obviously.

296
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:27,480
 And then you've got edges.

297
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:34,100
 And you can actually select by changing the selection type from vertices,

298
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:37,600
 which is the default, to edges, clicking that little button,

299
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:41,300
 and then I can click on the edges or select faces.

300
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:42,660
 Okay, like this.

301
00:17:42,660 --> 00:17:45,260
 And again, Blender has hotkeys for everything.

302
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:49,260
 So if you want to just know the hotkey, it's one, to do vertices select,

303
00:17:49,260 --> 00:17:52,280
 two, to do edge select, and then three, to do face select.

304
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:58,000
 Again, make sure you get my keyboard shortcut guide because it's a lot to remember.

305
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,740
 So I want to select the top of this cup here.

306
00:18:01,740 --> 00:18:03,820
 It's got a lid on it that I don't want.

307
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:08,600
 So in face select mode, selecting that face, I'm going to hit X.

308
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:12,280
 Now it's going to ask me, what part do you want to delete?

309
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:16,560
 Which might sound redundant, but there is quite a lot of times in Blender you want to delete,

310
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:21,520
 even though you've selected a bunch of faces, you want to delete the entire vertices of those faces.

311
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:27,380
 So we have to confirm, we want to delete just the faces from this list, and there we go.

312
00:18:27,380 --> 00:18:29,840
 So we've now got a cup with a hole in it.

313
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:30,720
 Great.

314
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:33,180
 But this cup will not do.

315
00:18:33,180 --> 00:18:34,940
 It is too paper thin.

316
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:37,320
 We could cut our finger on that edge there.

317
00:18:37,320 --> 00:18:39,980
 So we need to add some thickness to this cup.

318
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:45,680
 And while we could do this in edit mode, like you could select everything and extrude it in and whatnot,

319
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:51,340
 we don't need to because there is actually a function we can do to add thickness to anything

320
00:18:51,340 --> 00:18:53,080
 without needing to go into edit mode.

321
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:56,760
 And that is underneath your modifier stack here.

322
00:18:56,760 --> 00:18:59,380
 So if you click this little wrench, this will activate modifiers.

323
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:00,720
 Let's just drag this out.

324
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:05,760
 And if you click add modifier at the top there, you can see you've got a number of different options.

325
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:09,480
 And the one that we're looking for is solidify.

326
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:11,300
 So this is underneath the generate.

327
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:15,980
 And just in case this changes for a future version of Blender, you can also go to search at the top there,

328
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:18,900
 and you could just type in solidify from the list.

329
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:20,160
 Okay.

330
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:23,500
 So now, now that we've got that, you can see we've got a bunch of options,

331
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:26,640
 and you'll notice that our mesh now has some thickness to it.

332
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:28,640
 And I can change this thickness here.

333
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:32,500
 Now, watch what happens as I enter into edit mode.

334
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:41,540
 You will notice, as I select these faces here, I can't select this thick, this inside part of the cup there.

335
00:19:41,540 --> 00:19:47,360
 And that's an important point because modifiers are not actually adjusting the real mesh.

336
00:19:47,360 --> 00:19:49,920
 So the real mesh still exists here.

337
00:19:50,360 --> 00:19:54,720
 If I disable this from the viewport by clicking this little monitor button there,

338
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:57,040
 you'll see that my mesh has not changed.

339
00:19:57,040 --> 00:20:00,760
 This is just applying this effect after the fact.

340
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:02,780
 So it's basically in object mode.

341
00:20:02,780 --> 00:20:05,620
 It's taking whatever mesh that you give it, which is this,

342
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:09,180
 and then it's adding thickness to every face, essentially.

343
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:15,280
 So the reason this is so useful is that this is a non-destructive workflow.

344
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:20,040
 So I could, at any point, I could be doing a bunch of changes to my mug here.

345
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:22,080
 I could, you know, we'll get to all that later on.

346
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:23,780
 We could, you know, change the size and things.

347
00:20:23,780 --> 00:20:25,720
 And then later on, I can go, you know what?

348
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:27,200
 I don't, I think that's a little bit too thick.

349
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:28,180
 I'm going to change that.

350
00:20:28,520 --> 00:20:31,500
 And I can come back to that and very quickly adjust it.

351
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:35,960
 So non-destructive, and it's obviously very helpful for a lot of things.

352
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:37,160
 Okay.

353
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:42,840
 So I've given it some thickness, but I also want to round this corner here.

354
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:44,000
 That's way too jagged.

355
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:48,660
 Just like before I said, we want to, we're going to add in an extra layer on top,

356
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:49,620
 which adds more geometry.

357
00:20:49,620 --> 00:20:56,960
 And that is called your add, modify, generate subdivision surface.

358
00:20:57,520 --> 00:20:58,740
 So yeah, all the way down there.

359
00:20:58,740 --> 00:21:01,180
 You use it all the time, but it's like buried in there.

360
00:21:01,180 --> 00:21:01,980
 Okay.

361
00:21:01,980 --> 00:21:04,200
 So subdivision surface.

362
00:21:04,200 --> 00:21:07,440
 So this appears to have like screwed up the mesh.

363
00:21:07,440 --> 00:21:09,360
 It's like, well, what actually happened there?

364
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:12,740
 Let's just increase the levels viewport.

365
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:17,320
 So this is just increasing the number of times that it doubles the geometry.

366
00:21:17,320 --> 00:21:20,980
 So you can see, this is what we start with, right?

367
00:21:20,980 --> 00:21:21,940
 This is the start.

368
00:21:21,940 --> 00:21:23,120
 This is one level.

369
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:24,520
 Then this is two levels.

370
00:21:24,520 --> 00:21:25,960
 And then this is three levels.

371
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:27,880
 And every time you do it, it's doubling the geometry.

372
00:21:27,880 --> 00:21:31,500
 But you can see it's giving us the effect we want.

373
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:33,240
 It's smoothing out that edge there.

374
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:39,360
 But because of the way that it works, like basically, if you look at, let's go back to this.

375
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:42,560
 We've got this hard corner here, right?

376
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:44,320
 It's just like a 90 degree corner.

377
00:21:44,320 --> 00:21:47,120
 So let me get a little bit closer here so you can kind of see it.

378
00:21:47,120 --> 00:21:48,280
 Yeah.

379
00:21:48,280 --> 00:21:50,880
 It's like a 90 degree, perfectly like that.

380
00:21:50,880 --> 00:21:55,860
 So when you add in a subdivision surface, it's basically saying, okay, so there's a point here.

381
00:21:55,860 --> 00:21:58,020
 There is a point all the way over here.

382
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:03,270
 And there is a point that's going to go all the way, like it's so far, it's off the screen, but it's all the way down

383
00:22:03,270 --> 00:22:03,560
 there.

384
00:22:03,560 --> 00:22:06,820
 So what I need to do is I need to average out those points.

385
00:22:06,820 --> 00:22:09,500
 So take the middle point of those three.

386
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:14,180
 So essentially from here to somewhere around here.

387
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:17,740
 And I'm going to add in a point like here, right?

388
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:23,160
 And then if you do another level, like level two, it then places a point here and then another point here.

389
00:22:23,160 --> 00:22:27,960
 And then if you do another level, it places another point here, another point here, another point here.

390
00:22:27,960 --> 00:22:34,680
 And because there's two sides to this mug, there's like another one happening on that side, right?

391
00:22:34,680 --> 00:22:40,480
 So we've now just got this like really super like oval shaped edge like this, okay?

392
00:22:40,480 --> 00:22:43,240
 Which is why when I turn it on, we get this effect there.

393
00:22:43,760 --> 00:22:49,540
 So what we first of all need to do in edit mode with our mug here.

394
00:22:49,540 --> 00:22:54,320
 So I am in, actually it doesn't matter what mode I'm actually in here.

395
00:22:54,320 --> 00:23:01,280
 But what I need to do is essentially tighten an edge where that smoothing is happening.

396
00:23:01,280 --> 00:23:04,580
 So it's no longer happening from all the way here to smooth it all the way down to there.

397
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:06,780
 I want to happen at like to about there.

398
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:10,180
 So I need to add in more geometry onto this mesh here.

399
00:23:10,460 --> 00:23:13,520
 So to do that, we need to add a loop cut.

400
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:17,620
 That is how you add in another level of, another edge.

401
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:20,880
 So control R will add in this loop cut.

402
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:27,380
 I believe you can also find it from the mesh, like somewhere in here.

403
00:23:27,380 --> 00:23:28,920
 Yeah, there you go.

404
00:23:28,920 --> 00:23:29,780
 Edge.

405
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:32,300
 Loop cut and slide if you wanted to look it in there.

406
00:23:32,440 --> 00:23:33,940
 But it's, yeah, control R.

407
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:35,600
 That's another one you use all the time.

408
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:42,540
 So when I'm in, when I hit control R, it's saying, where do you want to place the cut in this mesh?

409
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:47,340
 And I could place it, you know, anywhere, anywhere between a face, essentially.

410
00:23:47,340 --> 00:23:49,320
 So I'm going to place it along this middle.

411
00:23:49,320 --> 00:23:50,680
 So you do a single click.

412
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:59,540
 And then it's saying, where do you want to place that vertically on this, this, this row of faces, essentially.

413
00:23:59,540 --> 00:24:04,480
 So you can see as I bring it towards the top there, it's tightening that edge there.

414
00:24:04,660 --> 00:24:06,500
 Okay, and that's exactly what I want.

415
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:09,640
 I want that edge to be much tighter than it was before.

416
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:12,760
 So that edge is now looking good.

417
00:24:12,760 --> 00:24:14,140
 But now what about this one at the bottom there?

418
00:24:14,140 --> 00:24:15,600
 This one's looking even worse.

419
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:16,700
 We've got a lot to fix here.

420
00:24:16,700 --> 00:24:19,280
 But first, I need to do another loop cut.

421
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:20,380
 So control R.

422
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:25,640
 And then again, single click to confirm I want it on that, that row of faces.

423
00:24:25,640 --> 00:24:32,820
 And then I'm just sliding it along until it's, you know, roughly about there at the bottom of my mug.

424
00:24:32,820 --> 00:24:33,380
 Okay.

425
00:24:34,240 --> 00:24:42,380
 And then you can see it's like this whole area here, this, this face here, it's become like a, like it's turned it into

426
00:24:42,380 --> 00:24:42,980
 a star.

427
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:49,420
 And not to get too complicated, but basically a subsurf modifier, it has to, has to know what to do.

428
00:24:49,420 --> 00:24:54,360
 And generally, if there's like faces that are made up of four vertices, this is getting complicated.

429
00:24:54,360 --> 00:24:56,640
 I can tell it knows what to do.

430
00:24:56,640 --> 00:24:57,920
 It makes it like nice and smooth.

431
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:03,220
 But the moment you have a face that is made up of more than four vertices, it's called an N-gon.

432
00:25:03,820 --> 00:25:05,560
 And it doesn't really know what to do with it.

433
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:08,500
 I mean, it does, but you don't know what it's going to do.

434
00:25:08,500 --> 00:25:10,820
 And it just can make things look a little ugly.

435
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:13,960
 N-gon is kind of like a dirty word in 3D space.

436
00:25:13,960 --> 00:25:15,780
 It's like, oh, you never want to have an N-gon.

437
00:25:15,780 --> 00:25:18,600
 They are actually useful and they, you know, do have a purpose.

438
00:25:18,600 --> 00:25:23,340
 But generally, generally speaking, they're good to avoid if you don't know what you're doing.

439
00:25:23,900 --> 00:25:25,920
 So, anyway, I should tell you what I'm doing.

440
00:25:25,920 --> 00:25:32,140
 The way to fix this is I need to add another loop that kind of just goes around here, right there.

441
00:25:32,140 --> 00:25:34,800
 So that way, I've only got from there to there.

442
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:39,280
 And then I can have my ugly star or whatever happening underneath my coffee mug.

443
00:25:39,700 --> 00:25:41,740
 So I need to add a loop cut.

444
00:25:41,740 --> 00:25:44,600
 But I can't add a loop cut on just a single face.

445
00:25:44,600 --> 00:25:52,240
 Instead, what I need to do is, it's called inset, to take a single face and then just like add in a loop around that

446
00:25:52,240 --> 00:25:52,980
 face.

447
00:25:53,320 --> 00:25:54,800
 And you do that by hitting I.

448
00:25:54,800 --> 00:25:59,300
 So I and then just like it's already active.

449
00:25:59,300 --> 00:26:02,280
 Again, this is the way I spent a while to explain it.

450
00:26:02,280 --> 00:26:07,020
 The moment you activate a tool, you're already in that selection state.

451
00:26:07,020 --> 00:26:12,580
 So I hit I and wherever my cursor is, it's now going like, where do you want to put this inset?

452
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:12,940
 Okay.

453
00:26:12,940 --> 00:26:15,520
 So I'm just moving it back and forth until I get it.

454
00:26:15,520 --> 00:26:18,600
 You know, how tight do I want this edge to look?

455
00:26:18,600 --> 00:26:22,020
 Something like that, maybe.

456
00:26:22,020 --> 00:26:23,980
 I mean, it's underneath the coffee mug.

457
00:26:23,980 --> 00:26:25,880
 It doesn't really matter that much.

458
00:26:25,880 --> 00:26:29,020
 But yeah, something like that.

459
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:30,260
 And there we go.

460
00:26:30,260 --> 00:26:32,200
 So you can see this looks a lot nicer.

461
00:26:32,200 --> 00:26:40,600
 And again, because we are in a non-destructive workflow, I've still got my solidify modifier at the top here, right?

462
00:26:40,600 --> 00:26:42,480
 I could change the thickness of this.

463
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:47,180
 I can make this a really chunky looking coffee mug or a really thin one.

464
00:26:47,180 --> 00:26:51,540
 And that is the benefit of a non-destructive workflow.

465
00:26:51,920 --> 00:26:57,220
 And then I can also change how high res do I want my coffee mug to look.

466
00:26:57,220 --> 00:26:59,860
 And this is the way to work in 3D.

467
00:26:59,860 --> 00:27:01,300
 This is the best way to do it.

468
00:27:01,300 --> 00:27:08,920
 Because, you know, if a client wants you to change something or whatnot, keeping in a flexible workflow is definitely a

469
00:27:08,920 --> 00:27:10,740
 good place to be.

470
00:27:11,480 --> 00:27:11,920
 Okay.

471
00:27:11,920 --> 00:27:18,260
 So we have created the start of a coffee mug and we have also created a donut.

472
00:27:18,260 --> 00:27:20,100
 Good time to save.

473
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:21,280
 Good time to save.

474
00:27:21,500 --> 00:27:27,080
 So file, save as, or shift, control, S, and then save as.

475
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:33,380
 So the way I like to save things, and you might want to copy this, is to, you come up with a name, you know, underscore

476
00:27:33,380 --> 00:27:36,480
 whatever, and then you put a number after it.

477
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:38,900
 And then you can actually see I've already saved my file.

478
00:27:39,780 --> 00:27:44,900
 I don't want to override my existing save in case I need to go back to it for whatever reason.

479
00:27:45,080 --> 00:27:46,500
 So I just want to add a number to it.

480
00:27:46,500 --> 00:27:51,180
 And in other softwares, you know, you have to come here and you have to type in the number, right?

481
00:27:51,180 --> 00:27:52,380
 Not Blender.

482
00:27:52,380 --> 00:27:54,620
 Blender has a hotkey for everything.

483
00:27:54,620 --> 00:28:00,720
 If you hit plus or minus on your numpad or on the little field here.

484
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:01,780
 Oh, you can't even see it.

485
00:28:01,780 --> 00:28:02,860
 I'm right over the top of it.

486
00:28:02,860 --> 00:28:03,420
 Right there.

487
00:28:03,640 --> 00:28:11,000
 This little field, these little plus and minus, it'll automatically add a number to the end of your file.

488
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:13,220
 So that's, yeah, really useful.

489
00:28:13,220 --> 00:28:16,260
 So I'm just going to add a 2 there, and I'm going to hit save.

490
00:28:16,260 --> 00:28:24,940
 It is time to make our coffee mug look like a coffee mug and not like a sad little cup.

491
00:28:24,940 --> 00:28:30,460
 So we could try to model this from our imagination.

492
00:28:30,460 --> 00:28:32,740
 Because like, hey, we all pick up coffee mugs.

493
00:28:32,860 --> 00:28:34,000
 We know what they look like.

494
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,400
 Or we think we know what they look like.

495
00:28:36,400 --> 00:28:42,600
 Something that you learn very quickly in 3D is that when you model something from imagination,

496
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:46,400
 it almost always comes out looking a little bit funny.

497
00:28:46,400 --> 00:28:51,900
 And the reason for that is that your brain is very good at like abstracting away the information

498
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:53,460
 that isn't important to it.

499
00:28:53,460 --> 00:28:57,480
 Like if you just close your eyes right now and just think of a doorknob.

500
00:28:57,480 --> 00:28:59,880
 Like what does a doorknob look like, right?

501
00:28:59,880 --> 00:29:02,060
 If you had to make one in Blender, what would it be?

502
00:29:02,080 --> 00:29:04,420
 It's like, okay, well, it's like a sphere bit.

503
00:29:04,420 --> 00:29:05,420
 You like grip it.

504
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:06,000
 You turn it.

505
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:07,680
 And there's like a cylinder thing at the back.

506
00:29:07,680 --> 00:29:09,060
 And there's a cylinder that connects it.

507
00:29:09,060 --> 00:29:11,000
 And that's kind of it.

508
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:17,440
 But then if you were to make that, you end up with something that looks really basic and boring and bad.

509
00:29:17,820 --> 00:29:24,160
 And then you go actually look at a real doorknob and you forget there's all this other detail that's just not important

510
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:24,960
 to your brain.

511
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:30,800
 Like the little lip inside, the little hole that's on there so you can put a key in to kind of like pry it open or like

512
00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:33,900
 a rim that kind of like cuts into it.

513
00:29:33,900 --> 00:29:36,340
 So there's like a separate seam around it.

514
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:38,080
 There's the privacy lock on the front.

515
00:29:38,080 --> 00:29:41,980
 All these things that just don't matter to you when you're turning a handle.

516
00:29:42,220 --> 00:29:46,060
 And so you forgot that they even existed because you never even saw it really.

517
00:29:46,060 --> 00:29:51,060
 So the same thing applies to even something as common as a coffee mug.

518
00:29:51,220 --> 00:29:54,600
 So it helps when possible to use reference images.

519
00:29:54,600 --> 00:30:01,220
 Now, you can get reference images from anywhere because we have the power of the Internet.

520
00:30:01,220 --> 00:30:07,060
 And you can type in handmade coffee mug and you can pick any mug you want to create.

521
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:10,220
 And there's a lot.

522
00:30:10,220 --> 00:30:16,480
 And, you know, if you want to pick one of these, if you want to just go on Google Images, you can pick one of these and

523
00:30:16,480 --> 00:30:17,360
 go for it.

524
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:22,740
 I'm going to give you an image that I've used, but you can, there's some really pretty ones here.

525
00:30:22,740 --> 00:30:30,780
 And I was actually shocked just whilst like clicking on some of these sites before.

526
00:30:30,780 --> 00:30:34,540
 I was like, oh, yeah, like that's a cute, cute little handmade mug.

527
00:30:34,540 --> 00:30:41,880
 And then you go like, oh, my gosh, $123.25.

528
00:30:41,880 --> 00:30:45,360
 And that's a discount from $145.

529
00:30:46,020 --> 00:30:50,340
 And then you think like, oh, it must be like a one of one, like, you know, a unique thing.

530
00:30:50,340 --> 00:30:52,240
 Like, no, there's like nine reviews.

531
00:30:52,240 --> 00:30:53,540
 Like people are buying it.

532
00:30:53,540 --> 00:30:56,920
 I'm like, I don't know.

533
00:30:56,920 --> 00:30:58,440
 I got to make more money or something.

534
00:30:58,440 --> 00:31:02,880
 Like, I don't know how people are buying $123 coffee mugs.

535
00:31:02,880 --> 00:31:06,660
 I think it's also like we forget what labor costs, you know.

536
00:31:06,660 --> 00:31:09,280
 Like you think like a coffee mug is like $2, right?

537
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:09,820
 Or a dollar.

538
00:31:09,820 --> 00:31:10,640
 Or it's free.

539
00:31:10,640 --> 00:31:12,780
 Like, it's just, there's so many of them.

540
00:31:12,780 --> 00:31:13,600
 It doesn't even matter.

541
00:31:13,740 --> 00:31:16,380
 But like, no, like to hand make something.

542
00:31:16,380 --> 00:31:23,940
 Like, obviously, any of these mugs, they take more than a few hours to make if you're making it by hand.

543
00:31:23,940 --> 00:31:27,820
 So like, yeah, $33 is probably like break even for them.

544
00:31:27,820 --> 00:31:29,640
 I don't, probably not even making any money.

545
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:32,020
 But it's just like, yeah.

546
00:31:32,160 --> 00:31:36,800
 Anyways, whole side topic of like the economy of handmade ceramic mugs.

547
00:31:36,800 --> 00:31:39,660
 But people are buying them for $123.

548
00:31:39,660 --> 00:31:40,920
 All right.

549
00:31:40,920 --> 00:31:44,360
 So all that is to say is I want you to get an image.

550
00:31:44,360 --> 00:31:47,640
 And the one I'm going to use, the link for this is below.

551
00:31:47,640 --> 00:31:50,480
 And it's on my website.

552
00:31:50,480 --> 00:31:52,860
 I've got all the links to everything we're going to be using in this tutorial.

553
00:31:53,500 --> 00:31:55,320
 But there's two ways to add images.

554
00:31:55,320 --> 00:32:00,140
 So one way is to go to the add menu and then just go image and then say reference.

555
00:32:00,140 --> 00:32:03,500
 And then you just pick wherever the reference is and you can drop it in.

556
00:32:03,500 --> 00:32:07,580
 The alternative way, and it's something that we didn't used to be able to do.

557
00:32:07,580 --> 00:32:13,220
 And that's to just drag over from wherever it is on your computer and then just drop it into Blender.

558
00:32:13,220 --> 00:32:16,660
 And it will recognize it as an image and it will bring it in as an image object.

559
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:19,160
 So either of those ways will work.

560
00:32:19,160 --> 00:32:20,220
 So this is the image.

561
00:32:20,220 --> 00:32:21,420
 This is the one we're going to use.

562
00:32:21,420 --> 00:32:26,000
 Now, if you quickly orbit around, you'll notice that it's come in at a funny angle.

563
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:29,460
 Now, I don't know why Blender does this.

564
00:32:29,460 --> 00:32:35,460
 I think it's because they think people want it locked to the perspective that they were at when they brought it in

565
00:32:35,460 --> 00:32:35,860
 there.

566
00:32:35,860 --> 00:32:37,420
 But I've never needed that.

567
00:32:37,420 --> 00:32:41,400
 So we just need to clear this rotation and then rotate it to the way we want.

568
00:32:41,400 --> 00:32:44,540
 So to clear the rotation, I'm going to hit Alt-R.

569
00:32:44,640 --> 00:32:48,920
 The alternative way, object, clear, rotation, Alt-R, same thing.

570
00:32:48,920 --> 00:32:50,800
 Now it's flat.

571
00:32:50,800 --> 00:32:57,660
 So now we need to rotate it, prop it upright so that it is flat on to, you know, how it should be relative to the scene.

572
00:32:57,660 --> 00:32:59,300
 So I need to rotate it.

573
00:32:59,300 --> 00:33:00,400
 So I'm going to hit R.

574
00:33:00,400 --> 00:33:03,640
 So we're now in freehand rotate mode.

575
00:33:03,640 --> 00:33:09,680
 And it's, again, it's relative to where we were looking at it, which is, yeah, this funny angle.

576
00:33:09,860 --> 00:33:16,830
 So if you remember from before, when we're in a move state, like rotate, scale, or grab, we can lock something to a

577
00:33:16,830 --> 00:33:22,280
 specific axis whilst we're in this mode by just tapping the letter of that axis.

578
00:33:22,280 --> 00:33:26,560
 And if you don't know the letter, you can see we really want it on this red line here.

579
00:33:26,560 --> 00:33:30,300
 And that red top right-hand corner, that is the X axis.

580
00:33:30,500 --> 00:33:31,840
 So I'm going to hit X.

581
00:33:31,840 --> 00:33:32,780
 There we go.

582
00:33:32,780 --> 00:33:35,540
 So now it is locked to that axis, which is good.

583
00:33:35,540 --> 00:33:37,280
 And now we want it to be 90 degrees.

584
00:33:37,280 --> 00:33:43,450
 So you could either hold down Control, and you can see in the top, what that does is that's now moving it in five

585
00:33:43,450 --> 00:33:44,920
 degree increments.

586
00:33:45,580 --> 00:33:50,540
 Or you could just type in the number 90 on your keyboard, and then hit Enter.

587
00:33:50,540 --> 00:33:51,500
 And there we go.

588
00:33:51,500 --> 00:33:56,220
 So that's how we bring it in and clear the rotation and set it up.

589
00:33:56,220 --> 00:33:57,640
 So, all right.

590
00:33:57,640 --> 00:33:59,100
 Now it's way too big.

591
00:33:59,100 --> 00:34:01,580
 So hotkey to scale something, S.

592
00:34:01,580 --> 00:34:03,160
 Okay, S to scale.

593
00:34:03,160 --> 00:34:04,920
 And now I'm just going to scale this down.

594
00:34:04,920 --> 00:34:06,560
 And I can't see my mug.

595
00:34:06,560 --> 00:34:07,640
 So my mug is behind it.

596
00:34:07,640 --> 00:34:09,700
 So let me just move my mug.

597
00:34:09,700 --> 00:34:10,940
 I'll move my mug forward.

598
00:34:10,940 --> 00:34:12,360
 So let's move it forward to there.

599
00:34:12,360 --> 00:34:13,620
 Okay.

600
00:34:14,400 --> 00:34:15,080
 All right.

601
00:34:15,080 --> 00:34:19,640
 And then now I'm going to look at it like front on.

602
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:20,020
 Okay.

603
00:34:20,020 --> 00:34:22,840
 So I can do that by clicking this little minus Y at the top there.

604
00:34:22,840 --> 00:34:23,480
 That'll do front on.

605
00:34:23,480 --> 00:34:26,400
 Or the hotkeys, because I use my numpad.

606
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:28,680
 For those of you blessed with a numpad, you'll like it.

607
00:34:28,680 --> 00:34:30,040
 You can hit numpad 1.

608
00:34:30,040 --> 00:34:30,780
 That's front view.

609
00:34:30,780 --> 00:34:32,040
 Numpad 3.

610
00:34:32,040 --> 00:34:32,960
 That's side view.

611
00:34:32,960 --> 00:34:34,280
 Or numpad 7.

612
00:34:34,280 --> 00:34:35,120
 That is top view.

613
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:37,140
 But I use those a lot.

614
00:34:37,140 --> 00:34:39,060
 But, again, you can use these if you don't have it.

615
00:34:39,060 --> 00:34:39,820
 Okay.

616
00:34:39,820 --> 00:34:40,460
 So front view.

617
00:34:40,460 --> 00:34:43,960
 And then I'm going to scroll in to zoom.

618
00:34:44,480 --> 00:34:46,660
 Shift middle mouse to pan to the side.

619
00:34:46,660 --> 00:34:48,680
 And now I'm going to move this over here.

620
00:34:48,680 --> 00:34:54,960
 And I'm going to scale this down again to get it roughly to be the size of my mug here.

621
00:34:54,960 --> 00:34:57,180
 Because I know that this mug is right relative to the donut.

622
00:34:57,180 --> 00:34:58,100
 So it should be good.

623
00:34:58,100 --> 00:35:03,860
 And then what I'm looking at for the size of this image here is I want the top of this

624
00:35:03,860 --> 00:35:07,140
 rim here to roughly match the bottom down there.

625
00:35:07,220 --> 00:35:09,340
 So this, like, I want that to match that.

626
00:35:09,340 --> 00:35:11,380
 So it's still a little bit too big.

627
00:35:11,380 --> 00:35:15,800
 And you'll notice I can't really easily do it from this angle.

628
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:16,700
 I mean, I can.

629
00:35:16,700 --> 00:35:22,540
 But the problem is that this photograph is taken with a camera from the real world where there

630
00:35:22,540 --> 00:35:23,180
 is perspective.

631
00:35:23,180 --> 00:35:25,100
 But we are looking in 3D.

632
00:35:25,100 --> 00:35:29,920
 And actually, when we're in an axis like this, the reason it all becomes flattened is because

633
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:31,400
 it is in perspective mode.

634
00:35:31,400 --> 00:35:34,820
 If you click that little button there, that'll actually put you back into perspective mode.

635
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:38,000
 And you can see how things get distorted and, like, angles and things.

636
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:42,640
 So the best reference is actually reference, which is in orthographic, like blueprints for

637
00:35:42,640 --> 00:35:42,960
 something.

638
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:44,880
 But you usually don't have that.

639
00:35:44,880 --> 00:35:47,760
 So you just go from something like this and just kind of best guess it.

640
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:48,840
 Anyways.

641
00:35:48,840 --> 00:35:49,340
 Okay.

642
00:35:49,340 --> 00:35:50,100
 Cool.

643
00:35:50,100 --> 00:35:54,880
 So that's roughly, roughly the right size of image.

644
00:35:54,880 --> 00:36:01,060
 Now we can model by going into edit mode by hitting tab, toggle in and out of edit mode.

645
00:36:01,060 --> 00:36:05,380
 And we can start to shape this to look like this mug here.

646
00:36:05,380 --> 00:36:10,020
 So the first thing you will notice is, like, the general broad shape of the mug is that

647
00:36:10,020 --> 00:36:12,840
 it actually kind of tapers towards the top.

648
00:36:12,840 --> 00:36:14,800
 Both lines are kind of tapered upwards.

649
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:20,220
 And then there's, like, a kind of a midpoint around about here where it starts to invert

650
00:36:20,220 --> 00:36:22,560
 and it goes in the other direction.

651
00:36:22,560 --> 00:36:22,920
 Okay.

652
00:36:22,920 --> 00:36:27,060
 So it's a very smooth object and perfect for using subsurf modifier, which is what we've

653
00:36:27,060 --> 00:36:27,600
 already got.

654
00:36:27,600 --> 00:36:33,880
 So to get that taper going up to the side, I want to shrink the top of the mug here just

655
00:36:33,880 --> 00:36:34,420
 a little bit.

656
00:36:34,600 --> 00:36:40,280
 So I'm going to hold down alt and I'm going to go into edge select mode at the top there

657
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:44,380
 and then alt and then just left click on that line.

658
00:36:44,380 --> 00:36:48,120
 Not on, like, like a middle point like here, it'll select a different edge, but just on

659
00:36:48,120 --> 00:36:48,880
 the middle there.

660
00:36:48,880 --> 00:36:53,920
 And then now I want to scale this in to just shrink the size just a little bit.

661
00:36:53,920 --> 00:36:55,840
 So I'm going to scale that to there.

662
00:36:56,640 --> 00:37:00,000
 And then I'm going to select the line underneath it and I'm going to do the same thing.

663
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:05,240
 So alt, click on that line, that edge, and then just scale it, you know, about halfway

664
00:37:05,240 --> 00:37:05,740
 there.

665
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:12,120
 Now I want to create this like widened point right here.

666
00:37:12,120 --> 00:37:16,660
 And you can see like from here, like there's, there's a lip at the bottom, but like from

667
00:37:16,660 --> 00:37:18,560
 here to here, and then it goes up.

668
00:37:18,700 --> 00:37:21,680
 I'd say probably 20% up the mug.

669
00:37:21,680 --> 00:37:23,360
 That's where that middle point is.

670
00:37:23,360 --> 00:37:26,960
 So this line is close, but I'd probably pull it up a little bit.

671
00:37:26,960 --> 00:37:30,520
 So I'm going to double tap G and that'll let me slide that edge there.

672
00:37:31,420 --> 00:37:34,260
 And I'll put it to, yeah, it's about there.

673
00:37:34,260 --> 00:37:38,500
 And now I want to pull it out to make it a little bit, a little bit thicker.

674
00:37:38,500 --> 00:37:45,060
 So I'm going to hit S and just pull that out to about there.

675
00:37:45,060 --> 00:37:47,880
 And now I can see this line here is not quite right.

676
00:37:47,880 --> 00:37:51,160
 So I'm going to scale this one out as well, just a little bit.

677
00:37:51,160 --> 00:37:52,260
 Okay.

678
00:37:52,260 --> 00:37:54,160
 That's roughly the right shape.

679
00:37:54,160 --> 00:37:54,680
 Okay.

680
00:37:54,680 --> 00:37:56,680
 Now this bit at the bottom here.

681
00:37:56,680 --> 00:37:58,100
 Okay.

682
00:37:58,100 --> 00:38:01,220
 So I can see already that I need a little bit more detail.

683
00:38:01,320 --> 00:38:03,840
 So I'm actually going to have to slide this line up here.

684
00:38:03,840 --> 00:38:08,680
 So I'm going to hit a double G to just kind of slide it up.

685
00:38:08,680 --> 00:38:10,180
 And I'm going to pull it to about there.

686
00:38:10,180 --> 00:38:14,540
 You can see now we've got kind of a more curved taper on that edge there.

687
00:38:14,540 --> 00:38:15,900
 And I'm going to hit S to scale.

688
00:38:15,900 --> 00:38:18,360
 I'm going to double tap G again.

689
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:23,320
 And I'm just by eye, just guessing to try to get this form here.

690
00:38:23,320 --> 00:38:29,280
 But you can see, you can very quickly start to shape any sort of object you want

691
00:38:29,280 --> 00:38:35,140
 by just moving, sliding lines around and scaling things.

692
00:38:35,140 --> 00:38:35,880
 Okay.

693
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:38,000
 So now obviously this face at the bottom here.

694
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:44,880
 So if I go into face select mode or by just hitting three, this has to be this little lip

695
00:38:44,880 --> 00:38:46,000
 at the bottom there.

696
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:46,660
 What do you call that?

697
00:38:46,800 --> 00:38:47,600
 I guess like a lip.

698
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:49,420
 It's probably a word for it.

699
00:38:49,420 --> 00:38:53,300
 All the pottery people watching this tutorial are going to tell me what it is.

700
00:38:53,300 --> 00:38:58,700
 And they'll also tell me about the price of manual labor and why that coffee mug was quite

701
00:38:58,700 --> 00:38:59,360
 well priced.

702
00:38:59,360 --> 00:38:59,960
 I'm sure.

703
00:38:59,960 --> 00:39:06,860
 Anyways, I'm going to, yeah, I'll scale it in a little bit.

704
00:39:06,860 --> 00:39:09,280
 I'm just trying to guess like where would it start?

705
00:39:09,580 --> 00:39:10,980
 But, you know, you just do it by eye.

706
00:39:10,980 --> 00:39:12,240
 I think about there.

707
00:39:12,240 --> 00:39:13,540
 Okay.

708
00:39:13,540 --> 00:39:17,740
 And now with this face here selected, I need more geometry because I want to actually pull

709
00:39:17,740 --> 00:39:19,640
 it out from that face there.

710
00:39:19,640 --> 00:39:22,180
 But I can't just like pull it down, right?

711
00:39:22,180 --> 00:39:23,180
 Because now it's stretching it.

712
00:39:23,180 --> 00:39:24,200
 I need more geometry.

713
00:39:24,200 --> 00:39:27,200
 And so that is called an extrusion.

714
00:39:27,620 --> 00:39:32,840
 And thankfully, the hotkey for extrusion is just the first letter of its name, which is

715
00:39:32,840 --> 00:39:34,800
 E, E for extrusion.

716
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:38,520
 And just like inset and everything else in Blender, the moment you tap it, you're now

717
00:39:38,520 --> 00:39:39,320
 in that mode.

718
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:40,820
 I can pull it upwards.

719
00:39:40,820 --> 00:39:41,900
 I could pull it downwards.

720
00:39:41,900 --> 00:39:43,940
 Obviously, downwards is what we want.

721
00:39:43,940 --> 00:39:46,700
 So I'm going to do a single click right there.

722
00:39:46,700 --> 00:39:48,040
 Okay.

723
00:39:48,040 --> 00:39:52,360
 And then now, if I go back and just look at it in object mode.

724
00:39:52,360 --> 00:39:56,420
 And by the way, if you want to see it like the donut, you want to make it look smooth,

725
00:39:56,420 --> 00:39:58,320
 you can as well by going to shade smooth.

726
00:39:58,320 --> 00:40:00,000
 And that's okay.

727
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:03,640
 But you can see that this line here, this is a much tighter line.

728
00:40:03,640 --> 00:40:04,040
 Okay.

729
00:40:04,040 --> 00:40:09,500
 And the reason that this is happening is, of course, because of subsurf modifier and the

730
00:40:09,500 --> 00:40:17,600
 way that it works, it's essentially going from here to here.

731
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:20,500
 And then kind of like, I guess, like a midpoint in the middle there.

732
00:40:20,500 --> 00:40:24,100
 And it's trying to taper from there all the way in.

733
00:40:24,200 --> 00:40:29,440
 But then also from this point here, it now has to taper from there to kind of smooth out

734
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:30,180
 that line.

735
00:40:30,180 --> 00:40:34,860
 So we end up with just kind of like this, like very smoothed out little nub.

736
00:40:34,860 --> 00:40:39,620
 But to fix it, we just have to add in more geometry so that it doesn't start the smooth

737
00:40:39,620 --> 00:40:40,800
 so much further up.

738
00:40:41,120 --> 00:40:45,360
 So to do that, we need another loop cut, which you might remember the hot key, or you can

739
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:49,280
 find it in the menu, edge, loop cut and slide, but it is control R.

740
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:50,400
 So control R.

741
00:40:50,400 --> 00:40:52,880
 When you're in this mode, where do you want to put it first?

742
00:40:52,880 --> 00:40:55,140
 So do a single click on this face here.

743
00:40:55,140 --> 00:40:55,660
 So click.

744
00:40:56,200 --> 00:40:58,760
 And then it's now saying, where do you want to slide it to?

745
00:40:58,760 --> 00:41:02,640
 So I'm going to slide one to the bottom, like so.

746
00:41:02,640 --> 00:41:04,680
 And then I need another one right above it.

747
00:41:04,680 --> 00:41:06,520
 So I did a click there to confirm it.

748
00:41:06,520 --> 00:41:07,960
 So I'm going to hit control R.

749
00:41:08,280 --> 00:41:11,160
 And now I'm placing it on the exact same place.

750
00:41:11,160 --> 00:41:12,640
 And I'm just going to pull it up.

751
00:41:12,640 --> 00:41:14,180
 And there we go.

752
00:41:14,180 --> 00:41:16,400
 And now you can see it's much tighter at the bottom there.

753
00:41:16,400 --> 00:41:17,600
 And it looks good.

754
00:41:17,600 --> 00:41:21,380
 And if you really wanted to clean up this bottom base there, you can see we kind of got that

755
00:41:21,380 --> 00:41:23,400
 like starfishy pattern happening.

756
00:41:23,400 --> 00:41:27,820
 You could do the same thing we did before, which is select that face there and then do an inset.

757
00:41:27,820 --> 00:41:29,720
 It's not really that essential.

758
00:41:29,720 --> 00:41:32,660
 No one's really going to notice it, but you might.

759
00:41:32,660 --> 00:41:34,200
 And maybe you want to clean it up.

760
00:41:34,200 --> 00:41:36,640
 So yeah, but that's pretty good.

761
00:41:36,640 --> 00:41:38,000
 That's great.

762
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:39,600
 That's a good start to the mug.

763
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:41,860
 It looks good.

764
00:41:41,860 --> 00:41:42,400
 Awesome.

765
00:41:42,400 --> 00:41:42,840
 Well done.

766
00:41:42,840 --> 00:41:48,000
 Now it's time for the challenge of making this handle.

767
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:56,240
 So you can see it starts about here and then obviously extrudes around and goes back into

768
00:41:56,240 --> 00:41:57,920
 itself and is joined here.

769
00:41:57,920 --> 00:41:59,240
 So how do we do this?

770
00:41:59,240 --> 00:42:04,600
 So basically, whenever there is a join and you can see there's like, you know, like a nice

771
00:42:04,600 --> 00:42:07,480
 like a bevel edge there kind of thing.

772
00:42:07,720 --> 00:42:10,820
 It has to physically be part of the geometry of this.

773
00:42:10,820 --> 00:42:15,140
 You can't just have it like existing out in space because then it won't appear to be joined

774
00:42:15,140 --> 00:42:15,580
 together.

775
00:42:15,580 --> 00:42:21,540
 So essentially, we have to find somewhere on this mesh to start the handle from.

776
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:26,100
 And that point is actually here, right here, which is why I've modeled it this way.

777
00:42:26,100 --> 00:42:28,180
 Right here is where I'm going to extrude out.

778
00:42:28,180 --> 00:42:33,920
 Now, before I show you how to extrude, I first of all want to show you, I'm going to do something

779
00:42:33,920 --> 00:42:35,080
 and it's going to mess it up.

780
00:42:35,080 --> 00:42:39,560
 And I'm going to then show you why we need to do something else first before we start extruding.

781
00:42:39,920 --> 00:42:44,700
 So if I was to extrude this out, we could do the whole handle here, get it back there and

782
00:42:44,700 --> 00:42:45,440
 make it look perfect.

783
00:42:45,440 --> 00:42:51,320
 And then later on, look at our mug and go, oh no, what is going on?

784
00:42:51,720 --> 00:42:57,700
 The reason this happens is because of, you might remember, the solidifier modifier, which

785
00:42:57,700 --> 00:43:00,760
 these modifiers, they're not part of the mesh, right?

786
00:43:00,940 --> 00:43:05,420
 It's happening after the fact.

787
00:43:05,420 --> 00:43:09,560
 So it's adding a thickness to any mesh that we have here.

788
00:43:09,560 --> 00:43:13,180
 And it's now adding a thickness to the actual handle itself.

789
00:43:13,180 --> 00:43:15,400
 So how do we fix that?

790
00:43:15,400 --> 00:43:22,540
 We fix that by making this go from a modifier to real, like actually bake it into the mesh

791
00:43:22,540 --> 00:43:24,200
 so that it's actually real geometry.

792
00:43:24,200 --> 00:43:30,900
 And the way you do that is, first of all, let me just undo all of those controls.

793
00:43:30,940 --> 00:43:35,580
 Z of course, the way you do it is by going to the modifier and then in the little drop

794
00:43:35,580 --> 00:43:39,700
 down in the top there, right next to that little camera icon, there is an apply.

795
00:43:39,700 --> 00:43:42,080
 So when you click that, it's disappeared.

796
00:43:42,080 --> 00:43:47,100
 And now when you go into edit mode though, you can see all that geometry that wasn't there

797
00:43:47,100 --> 00:43:48,680
 before is now actually part of it.

798
00:43:48,680 --> 00:43:50,920
 And you can see it if you turn off the sub surf modifier.

799
00:43:50,920 --> 00:43:56,840
 So I can now make changes to the inner part of my model there without it having affecting

800
00:43:56,840 --> 00:43:58,140
 the rest of it.

801
00:43:58,140 --> 00:44:01,540
 So it's actual real mesh that we can manipulate.

802
00:44:01,540 --> 00:44:05,240
 Obviously, the downside is it's no longer non-destructive.

803
00:44:05,240 --> 00:44:09,160
 So that slider that we had for changing the thickness of the mug, we no longer have the

804
00:44:09,160 --> 00:44:09,460
 slider.

805
00:44:09,460 --> 00:44:13,640
 It would just mean if we wanted to change the thickness of the mug, we would have to like

806
00:44:13,640 --> 00:44:16,640
 do it ourselves, like selecting all that stuff and doing it.

807
00:44:16,640 --> 00:44:17,500
 But that's fine.

808
00:44:17,500 --> 00:44:22,000
 You have to break the workflow eventually to add in customization.

809
00:44:22,440 --> 00:44:28,380
 So anyways, now that I've got this, now when I select this face here, I can start to do the

810
00:44:28,380 --> 00:44:28,740
 extrusion.

811
00:44:28,740 --> 00:44:31,900
 So importantly, make sure you're selecting the right face.

812
00:44:31,900 --> 00:44:36,640
 If you were to select this one by mistake and then start trying to do your handle and then

813
00:44:36,640 --> 00:44:40,320
 like twist it around, it would start to get screwed up very, very quickly.

814
00:44:40,460 --> 00:44:46,240
 So make sure that you've got this red line that's like intersecting your mug there, that it

815
00:44:46,240 --> 00:44:48,180
 is, yeah, like it's that one.

816
00:44:48,180 --> 00:44:50,240
 It's like bang on to the middle.

817
00:44:50,240 --> 00:44:55,520
 And you can actually check if you go into top view mode, it should be directly in the middle

818
00:44:55,520 --> 00:44:57,200
 of the mug there.

819
00:44:57,700 --> 00:44:57,940
 Okay.

820
00:44:57,940 --> 00:45:02,940
 So with that selected, I'm going to go into front view mode with a numpad one or hitting

821
00:45:02,940 --> 00:45:04,240
 that little green thing.

822
00:45:04,240 --> 00:45:09,920
 And then I'm going to hit again, the hotkey that we use for extrusion is E to extrude

823
00:45:09,920 --> 00:45:10,140
 out.

824
00:45:10,140 --> 00:45:16,520
 Now, when you do that, it's using the normals, the hidden, it's like the hidden direction

825
00:45:16,520 --> 00:45:19,060
 of each of the faces that are on your mesh.

826
00:45:19,060 --> 00:45:19,440
 Okay.

827
00:45:19,440 --> 00:45:21,600
 So it's kind of extruding it out like that.

828
00:45:21,600 --> 00:45:27,080
 For most of you watching this, the extrusion there worked perfectly, but every now and then

829
00:45:27,080 --> 00:45:29,180
 you go to extrude something and it doesn't work.

830
00:45:29,180 --> 00:45:33,600
 And it's because there's something wrong with the normals of the face that you tried to extrude

831
00:45:33,600 --> 00:45:33,880
 from.

832
00:45:33,880 --> 00:45:38,300
 Basically, it would just mean you would have to clear the normals first.

833
00:45:38,300 --> 00:45:43,660
 So basically you would select the face, then hit shift N and it would clear normals.

834
00:45:43,660 --> 00:45:48,860
 I've got, by the way, throughout this tutorial, you're going to run into a lot of problems that

835
00:45:48,860 --> 00:45:50,920
 I might not be able to explain to you.

836
00:45:50,920 --> 00:45:55,900
 So I've actually created a troubleshooting guide called the unstuck yourself guide.

837
00:45:55,960 --> 00:45:57,700
 So the link for that is below as well.

838
00:45:57,700 --> 00:46:01,900
 But this is just like a database of all the problems you might run into whilst you're doing

839
00:46:01,900 --> 00:46:02,520
 this tutorial.

840
00:46:02,520 --> 00:46:07,880
 Because we noticed on the Blender Guru Discord, link for that is below as well, where you can

841
00:46:07,880 --> 00:46:10,000
 ask questions and share your renders and stuff.

842
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:12,600
 A lot of people are asking the same questions.

843
00:46:12,600 --> 00:46:17,640
 So we put together this like FAQ troubleshooting guide and please make use of that.

844
00:46:17,640 --> 00:46:21,700
 So there'll probably be, when you're modeling things, people will run into issues.

845
00:46:21,840 --> 00:46:24,440
 So just have a look at that if you run into an issue first.

846
00:46:24,440 --> 00:46:30,280
 Anyways, sorry to really painfully slowly do this, but we've extruded out a little bit.

847
00:46:30,280 --> 00:46:33,160
 Now I'm going to move this up.

848
00:46:33,160 --> 00:46:36,980
 So just grabbing, so G to grab, I'm going to move up.

849
00:46:36,980 --> 00:46:41,460
 And I might actually now, I'm going to tab out so that I can select my image.

850
00:46:41,460 --> 00:46:42,380
 So I'm in object mode.

851
00:46:42,700 --> 00:46:44,500
 And I'm actually just going to move this over.

852
00:46:44,500 --> 00:46:49,980
 So G and then X, move it over to be about there.

853
00:46:49,980 --> 00:46:50,700
 Okay.

854
00:46:50,700 --> 00:46:57,340
 And that'll just enable me to kind of see relative to the mug where it should go.

855
00:46:57,340 --> 00:46:58,200
 Like what is the shape?

856
00:46:58,200 --> 00:47:01,340
 I could do it by eye, but I figure I might as well just, it's right there.

857
00:47:01,340 --> 00:47:02,800
 I might as well just grab it and pull it over.

858
00:47:02,800 --> 00:47:06,420
 So I need to position this about here.

859
00:47:06,420 --> 00:47:08,560
 But you can see it's kind of getting like skewed, right?

860
00:47:09,060 --> 00:47:14,020
 And that is because it's, it's obviously coming in at like a, like this slanted angle.

861
00:47:14,020 --> 00:47:17,840
 So with that face selected, I need to rotate it.

862
00:47:17,840 --> 00:47:19,800
 So I'm going to hit R to rotate like so.

863
00:47:19,800 --> 00:47:21,180
 Okay.

864
00:47:21,180 --> 00:47:22,180
 So like that.

865
00:47:22,180 --> 00:47:23,900
 All right.

866
00:47:23,900 --> 00:47:27,500
 And I might just trying to think.

867
00:47:27,500 --> 00:47:28,400
 All right.

868
00:47:28,400 --> 00:47:32,600
 I definitely need a loop cut because obviously that bit that's coming out looking like a nub

869
00:47:32,600 --> 00:47:34,100
 on our mug there.

870
00:47:34,100 --> 00:47:38,540
 So I'm going to hit control R to create a loop cut and I'm going to pull it down so that

871
00:47:38,540 --> 00:47:41,620
 I can see it's like where it, where it should join basically at the base there.

872
00:47:41,620 --> 00:47:42,480
 All right.

873
00:47:42,480 --> 00:47:42,720
 Great.

874
00:47:42,720 --> 00:47:46,240
 And now face select mode, select that face again.

875
00:47:46,240 --> 00:47:49,320
 And now we just have to keep extruding around.

876
00:47:49,320 --> 00:47:53,520
 But what I want to do is I want to scale, just make the handle look a little bit thinner.

877
00:47:53,520 --> 00:47:56,000
 So I'm going to select this top edge here.

878
00:47:56,000 --> 00:48:00,720
 I'm going to double tap G just so that I can slide it downwards a little bit to just make

879
00:48:00,720 --> 00:48:06,400
 it a little bit thinner because that handle obviously doesn't look as, as fat as, as what

880
00:48:06,400 --> 00:48:06,900
 we had there.

881
00:48:06,900 --> 00:48:08,120
 So, all right.

882
00:48:08,120 --> 00:48:11,120
 So face select mode now, and now I'm going to start to extrude.

883
00:48:11,120 --> 00:48:15,360
 So another extrusion, G to reposition, R to rotate it.

884
00:48:15,360 --> 00:48:20,580
 And I just want to rotate it so that it kind of follows the contours of, of this.

885
00:48:20,580 --> 00:48:23,840
 So I'm going to be rotating it like this kind of angle as we go around.

886
00:48:24,360 --> 00:48:30,620
 So now E to do another extrusion, click, G, click, R.

887
00:48:30,620 --> 00:48:36,460
 Now, by the way, there is another way to extrude, which is actually kind of perfect for this.

888
00:48:37,000 --> 00:48:46,000
 But if you hold down control and then right click, it will not only extrude, but it will also rotate the faces so that

889
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:47,220
 they align.

890
00:48:47,220 --> 00:48:55,000
 And it can be a little helpful, a bit more easier than doing this like E, G, R, S, G, R.

891
00:48:55,220 --> 00:49:02,540
 Like, so just holding down control and then right click will just place these little points and will rotate them so

892
00:49:02,540 --> 00:49:05,180
 that you don't have to do all that rotation yourself.

893
00:49:05,180 --> 00:49:10,410
 It really only works for these kind of appendages like this, but might as well use it because it's a very handy way to

894
00:49:10,410 --> 00:49:10,920
 do it.

895
00:49:11,440 --> 00:49:12,240
 Okay, cool.

896
00:49:12,240 --> 00:49:14,220
 So now, now the tricky bit.

897
00:49:14,220 --> 00:49:15,400
 It's not really that tricky.

898
00:49:15,400 --> 00:49:18,380
 It's, it's relatively easy to solve, but it's, it's a conundrum, right?

899
00:49:18,380 --> 00:49:23,340
 Like, how do we make this mesh with the rest of the mesh?

900
00:49:23,340 --> 00:49:31,040
 And you do that by, like, the wrong way to do this would be to just like, kind of like smush them together, right?

901
00:49:31,040 --> 00:49:33,780
 And this is kind of like early 3D stuff.

902
00:49:33,780 --> 00:49:38,060
 Like a lot of stuff was like this, this kind of mashed stuff and intersected it together.

903
00:49:38,240 --> 00:49:41,700
 But then you don't get that edge there where it, like, catches the light.

904
00:49:41,700 --> 00:49:47,120
 And the only way to really get that is to actually physically, like, join this mesh together.

905
00:49:47,120 --> 00:49:50,620
 So this face here has to be connected to this face somehow.

906
00:49:50,620 --> 00:49:58,850
 And the easiest way in this case to do is to, first of all, we can see that this line here is, like, cutting into the

907
00:49:58,850 --> 00:50:01,520
 middle of this, this, this cup here, right?

908
00:50:01,520 --> 00:50:08,820
 So we really need to just add a line that goes around there to make this, this area here, like, one face to match this,

909
00:50:08,820 --> 00:50:11,300
 sorry, this, this, this handle.

910
00:50:11,300 --> 00:50:18,620
 So I'm going to add that line by hitting Control-R, loop cut, click, and then I'm going to slide it up to be, you know,

911
00:50:18,620 --> 00:50:20,100
 roughly about there.

912
00:50:20,100 --> 00:50:20,680
 That's fine.

913
00:50:21,360 --> 00:50:25,940
 And then, now, because I've intersected it, that went too far.

914
00:50:25,940 --> 00:50:28,040
 So I'm holding down Z.

915
00:50:28,040 --> 00:50:33,240
 Now, this is a way of quickly changing the mode that you're viewing something in.

916
00:50:33,240 --> 00:50:37,620
 And we'll get to these other two once we get to materials, rendering and stuff.

917
00:50:37,620 --> 00:50:42,500
 But for now, if you just swap to wireframe mode, this will show you through the mesh.

918
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:49,120
 There is another way to see through a mesh, and that is to, at the top there, that little X-ray or Alt-Z is a hotkey

919
00:50:49,120 --> 00:50:49,820
 for it.

920
00:50:49,820 --> 00:50:51,900
 But that's, that's another way to do it.

921
00:50:51,900 --> 00:50:55,620
 But, yeah, you can see that's, that's wireframe and then, oh, hang on a second.

922
00:50:55,620 --> 00:50:57,120
 Nope, that's, that's X-ray.

923
00:50:57,120 --> 00:50:58,380
 This is wireframe.

924
00:50:58,380 --> 00:51:00,080
 But, yeah, they're, they're pretty similar.

925
00:51:00,080 --> 00:51:05,320
 Anyways, so I'm going to select my, my face here, and I'm just going to pull it out.

926
00:51:05,320 --> 00:51:07,440
 Just so that I can just, just grab it, basically.

927
00:51:07,440 --> 00:51:09,480
 And now I can go back to my solid view as before.

928
00:51:09,480 --> 00:51:10,500
 Okay.

929
00:51:10,840 --> 00:51:15,760
 So I touched on this briefly before, but like a mesh is made up of these individual points, right?

930
00:51:15,760 --> 00:51:18,880
 This is what you're seeing when you're in a vertice mode, these individual points.

931
00:51:18,880 --> 00:51:25,020
 And each of those points, when you have four together, three or more, actually, you can create a face.

932
00:51:25,020 --> 00:51:26,840
 And so that's what that is.

933
00:51:26,840 --> 00:51:31,560
 So essentially, I need to create a new face for this section right here.

934
00:51:31,560 --> 00:51:32,360
 So let's do that now.

935
00:51:32,360 --> 00:51:37,060
 So I'm going to select those two, and I've just selected them by holding down a shift like this.

936
00:51:37,060 --> 00:51:40,320
 And then I'm just going to hit F, F for face.

937
00:51:40,680 --> 00:51:41,060
 Okay.

938
00:51:41,060 --> 00:51:44,360
 And then I can do the same on this side, like that.

939
00:51:44,360 --> 00:51:47,140
 And then I'm going to do the same on the top.

940
00:51:47,140 --> 00:51:50,860
 And then I'm going to do the same underneath.

941
00:51:50,860 --> 00:51:53,020
 Okay.

942
00:51:53,020 --> 00:51:54,100
 All right.

943
00:51:54,100 --> 00:52:00,690
 So I've done it, but, and I did it deliberately this way, because I need to show you the, the reason for this, this

944
00:52:00,690 --> 00:52:01,340
 issue here.

945
00:52:01,520 --> 00:52:04,360
 So we've got, yeah, we've, we've, we've got the faces.

946
00:52:04,360 --> 00:52:09,830
 So they should be going smoothly across here, but because of the way meshes work and the way they try to, sorry, the

947
00:52:09,830 --> 00:52:15,580
 way smooth shading works is, it does it based on like a continuous flow of faces.

948
00:52:16,080 --> 00:52:23,580
 And you will have noticed that when we created this, there was actually a face that is inside here before we joined it.

949
00:52:23,580 --> 00:52:23,820
 Okay.

950
00:52:23,820 --> 00:52:30,220
 Which, and I'll, I'll show you if we go into here, this area here, you should see there is a face that is right here.

951
00:52:30,340 --> 00:52:35,820
 So because that exists, the smooth shader doesn't actually know that it's supposed to continue on because there's a

952
00:52:35,820 --> 00:52:37,060
 face that goes down.

953
00:52:37,060 --> 00:52:39,140
 So it's now kind of mixed.

954
00:52:39,140 --> 00:52:44,140
 It doesn't know, like it has to create a smooth shading here, but then also one on the top there.

955
00:52:44,140 --> 00:52:50,280
 And so it's done this weird hybrid thing where it's like, it's tried to do it, but there's clearly something going on

956
00:52:50,280 --> 00:52:51,320
 underneath.

957
00:52:51,320 --> 00:52:53,380
 So what we need to do is just delete that face.

958
00:52:53,380 --> 00:52:57,100
 So again, in face select mode, you select that interface there.

959
00:52:57,200 --> 00:53:03,020
 And by the way, when you're working in 3D, this is a really common thing beginners do, is they select the wrong face.

960
00:53:03,020 --> 00:53:08,180
 So you can see how quickly that, like, they're right next to each other, that point there, right?

961
00:53:08,180 --> 00:53:11,480
 But selecting one or the other, it just happens all the time.

962
00:53:11,480 --> 00:53:16,270
 So a thing that a lot of 3D artists do is when they've selected something, before they delete it, you just do a quick

963
00:53:16,270 --> 00:53:17,080
 orbit around.

964
00:53:17,080 --> 00:53:19,260
 And that'll just like break the perspective.

965
00:53:19,260 --> 00:53:23,880
 And just to verify that that is the actual one I mean to select.

966
00:53:23,880 --> 00:53:24,460
 Okay.

967
00:53:24,620 --> 00:53:27,380
 Then I'm going to hit X, and I'm going to say faces.

968
00:53:27,380 --> 00:53:28,240
 Okay.

969
00:53:28,240 --> 00:53:30,000
 But it's not just that one, right?

970
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:30,960
 Like that.

971
00:53:30,960 --> 00:53:33,600
 Actually, that should be fixed soon, but anyway, I'll show you how.

972
00:53:33,600 --> 00:53:36,560
 And then there's one on the inside here as well, okay?

973
00:53:36,560 --> 00:53:39,360
 Because when we joined it, there was a face here, and there was a face over there.

974
00:53:39,360 --> 00:53:43,080
 So I'm now going to delete that face by hitting X, and then say faces.

975
00:53:43,080 --> 00:53:44,060
 Okay.

976
00:53:44,400 --> 00:53:45,500
 Now that's done.

977
00:53:45,500 --> 00:53:48,360
 This should be correct.

978
00:53:48,360 --> 00:53:53,840
 But the reason it's not is, one, I think if I need to right-click and say shade smooth, and that has fixed it.

979
00:53:53,840 --> 00:54:03,180
 What can happen sometimes is when you've fixed up some geometry, you might need to recalculate the normals.

980
00:54:03,180 --> 00:54:10,510
 And again, to bring up the normals, there's a secret hidden line that every face on your object has, and they're always

981
00:54:10,510 --> 00:54:11,680
 pointing outward like that.

982
00:54:11,680 --> 00:54:17,030
 But when you've created new mesh and you've kind of fixed things or whatnot, you can end up with one of these lines

983
00:54:17,030 --> 00:54:18,800
 that goes the wrong direction.

984
00:54:19,360 --> 00:54:21,520
 And I'll show you that, actually, if I just do this.

985
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:24,100
 That's what it might look like.

986
00:54:24,100 --> 00:54:28,360
 You get this weird, like, black shading artifacts that's very ugly.

987
00:54:28,360 --> 00:54:34,740
 So to fix that, you select your entire mesh, and then you hit Shift-N, and that will recalculate it.

988
00:54:34,740 --> 00:54:37,860
 And now you can see the smooth shading is correct.

989
00:54:37,860 --> 00:54:43,770
 And just to show you quickly as well, the way I did it just then, where we, like, created it, and then we deleted the

990
00:54:43,770 --> 00:54:44,380
 faces.

991
00:54:44,380 --> 00:54:45,300
 Don't do this.

992
00:54:45,300 --> 00:54:45,960
 I'm just going to show you.

993
00:54:46,280 --> 00:54:52,300
 Like, actually, what you would do, because it's not this tedious normally, if you had this problem, you would actually

994
00:54:52,300 --> 00:54:54,440
 select this face and this face first.

995
00:54:54,440 --> 00:54:56,420
 You delete them ahead of time.

996
00:54:56,420 --> 00:55:03,880
 And then, actually, and there's a faster way to have created this join here, you can select that entire edge loop with

997
00:55:03,880 --> 00:55:06,420
 Alt, then Shift-Alt, select that.

998
00:55:06,420 --> 00:55:13,360
 And now I could right-click and say Bridge Edge Loops, which you only can do if you've got the exact same number of

999
00:55:13,360 --> 00:55:15,620
 vertices on one side as the other.

1000
00:55:15,880 --> 00:55:22,370
 But I wanted to show you physically creating those points with vertices, because that is a really common way to model,

1001
00:55:22,370 --> 00:55:24,000
 to join things together.

1002
00:55:24,000 --> 00:55:26,740
 You select the four vertices, you create a new face.

1003
00:55:26,740 --> 00:55:28,180
 Anyways, look at that.

1004
00:55:28,180 --> 00:55:31,020
 We've got what looks like a handle coming out of the mug.

1005
00:55:31,020 --> 00:55:38,260
 Now, if we look at our reference here, we've got it pretty close, but you can see that there's, like, it's sharp, right?

1006
00:55:38,260 --> 00:55:41,080
 There's clearly, like, a crease here and a crease here.

1007
00:55:41,080 --> 00:55:45,100
 And we can do that just by adding in more loop cuts.

1008
00:55:45,100 --> 00:55:49,320
 So I'm going to add in, well, I guess there's two ways to do it.

1009
00:55:49,320 --> 00:55:51,760
 One, I can actually just select this edge here.

1010
00:55:51,760 --> 00:55:56,280
 So I'm going down Alt, just select that, and then double-tap G, and I can bring that in.

1011
00:55:56,280 --> 00:56:00,340
 But when I do that, this area becomes a little bit straight.

1012
00:56:01,320 --> 00:56:02,620
 Maybe not what I wanted.

1013
00:56:02,620 --> 00:56:03,980
 Let's have a look at front mode.

1014
00:56:03,980 --> 00:56:09,100
 Yeah, I might want a little bit more detail there, in which case I would add a loop cut.

1015
00:56:09,100 --> 00:56:10,980
 But, you know, it's really up to you.

1016
00:56:10,980 --> 00:56:15,200
 You might be able to just, you know, move this around and just kind of shape it.

1017
00:56:15,200 --> 00:56:17,640
 Like, look, we're really, we're learning Blender.

1018
00:56:17,640 --> 00:56:19,440
 That's really the goal of this.

1019
00:56:19,780 --> 00:56:25,280
 It's not necessarily to make a physically accurate mug.

1020
00:56:25,280 --> 00:56:30,420
 I don't want to overcomplicate things and make things feel like you have to do everything perfectly.

1021
00:56:30,420 --> 00:56:33,900
 But I also don't want to end up with, like, a trash result and then everyone be like,

1022
00:56:33,900 --> 00:56:36,180
 well, we did all that effort and now it looks garbage.

1023
00:56:36,180 --> 00:56:39,200
 But, you know, you could spend a lot of effort.

1024
00:56:39,200 --> 00:56:42,540
 You know, you could even try and get, like, if you look at, you know,

1025
00:56:42,540 --> 00:56:45,580
 there would be probably, like, a little line here.

1026
00:56:45,580 --> 00:56:47,480
 Like, the kind of, you know what I mean?

1027
00:56:47,480 --> 00:56:50,880
 Like, on the inner part of the thing, you could try and create that.

1028
00:56:50,880 --> 00:56:52,400
 You could really add a lot of detail.

1029
00:56:52,400 --> 00:56:55,480
 The only thing I'm really going to fix is right here where it's pinching.

1030
00:56:55,480 --> 00:56:59,520
 I'm just going to select that edge there and I'm going to double tap G.

1031
00:56:59,520 --> 00:57:00,940
 I'm just going to slide it up.

1032
00:57:00,940 --> 00:57:03,020
 So there's just a little less pinching.

1033
00:57:03,020 --> 00:57:09,460
 And then this part here, like, you can see the shape of this face is being very distorted, right?

1034
00:57:09,460 --> 00:57:14,940
 You generally want to keep things as close to a squarish shape as possible, generally.

1035
00:57:16,000 --> 00:57:19,740
 And so I'm just going to select this edge at the bottom there, double tap G,

1036
00:57:19,740 --> 00:57:23,840
 and I'm just going to pull it out just to relieve a little bit of the tension there.

1037
00:57:23,840 --> 00:57:29,700
 And, I mean, that's, I mean, you can, yeah, there's obviously, there's, like, a tighter line there.

1038
00:57:29,700 --> 00:57:31,100
 But this will do.

1039
00:57:31,100 --> 00:57:35,840
 This is enough to be perceived as a coffee mug.

1040
00:57:35,840 --> 00:57:40,140
 Now, if you want to just move ahead with the tutorial and get on to the next part,

1041
00:57:40,200 --> 00:57:45,100
 which is going to be on materials and lighting, you can do that.

1042
00:57:45,360 --> 00:57:48,320
 However, if you want a little bit of a challenge, like a little bit of a homework,

1043
00:57:48,320 --> 00:57:52,280
 where you can try to use some skills, but by yourself,

1044
00:57:52,280 --> 00:57:59,440
 you will see, looking at the photo here, that we also have a saucer, a little tiny plate for our mug.

1045
00:57:59,440 --> 00:58:03,140
 Now, I didn't include that in my final image.

1046
00:58:03,580 --> 00:58:05,760
 I just had the coffee mug just sitting on the table.

1047
00:58:05,760 --> 00:58:11,480
 But this is a great, very small thing that you could try to model by yourself,

1048
00:58:11,480 --> 00:58:16,420
 using the skills that you've just learned to create something new.

1049
00:58:16,500 --> 00:58:19,380
 So, how would you go about adding that saucer?

1050
00:58:19,380 --> 00:58:21,200
 You could have a try at it.

1051
00:58:21,200 --> 00:58:24,120
 It's a completely separate object, obviously, to your mug here.

1052
00:58:24,120 --> 00:58:27,640
 So, even if you screw it up, worst case scenario is you just delete it.

1053
00:58:27,640 --> 00:58:32,440
 And then, you know, you'll still end up with something that I actually had for my final result,

1054
00:58:32,440 --> 00:58:34,220
 which was a mug without a saucer.

1055
00:58:34,220 --> 00:58:40,800
 But if you want to add that in, it'll be a way to kind of take some of the lessons that you've picked up already

1056
00:58:40,800 --> 00:58:46,080
 and then try to remember them and the different steps and the things required to create it.

1057
00:58:46,080 --> 00:58:51,260
 Because whilst you've just created something now, it's the best time to try to recall that memory

1058
00:58:51,260 --> 00:58:54,540
 so that in the future, maybe in a few days or a few weeks from now,

1059
00:58:54,540 --> 00:59:00,040
 when you try to do something yourself, it'll be a lot more readily available to recall that information.

1060
00:59:00,040 --> 00:59:03,040
 So, if you want to have a go at doing that, feel free to.

1061
00:59:03,040 --> 00:59:05,120
 Otherwise, join me in the next part.

1062
00:59:05,120 --> 00:59:08,560
 Welcome back.

1063
00:59:08,560 --> 00:59:12,040
 In this part, we are going to be making the icing.

1064
00:59:12,040 --> 00:59:14,780
 Yes, the exciting icing for our donut.

1065
00:59:14,780 --> 00:59:18,440
 But first, we do have quite a lot of objects going on in our scene.

1066
00:59:18,440 --> 00:59:21,440
 We've got a camera, lamp, we've got an object, an image and stuff.

1067
00:59:21,440 --> 00:59:23,620
 We should try to organize some things.

1068
00:59:23,620 --> 00:59:26,120
 And some of you might have noticed, in the top right-hand corner,

1069
00:59:26,120 --> 00:59:29,500
 you've got a list of all of your objects, right?

1070
00:59:29,500 --> 00:59:32,080
 This is called your outliner.

1071
00:59:32,080 --> 00:59:38,180
 And it is a, like a spreadsheet version of all the objects that are in your scene.

1072
00:59:38,180 --> 00:59:41,760
 And we're going to use it later on to, like, organize, like, create collections,

1073
00:59:41,760 --> 00:59:43,760
 so it's, like, easier to see and organize things.

1074
00:59:43,760 --> 00:59:48,300
 But the first thing we need to do is just add names to things that make sense.

1075
00:59:48,300 --> 00:59:51,940
 So, for example, our mug here is currently called Cylinder.

1076
00:59:51,940 --> 00:59:55,400
 So, if we had a big scene with lots of objects going on, we can't find it.

1077
00:59:55,400 --> 00:59:57,360
 So, it's a good habit to name your objects.

1078
00:59:57,360 --> 00:59:59,280
 So, there's a number of different ways you could name something.

1079
01:00:00,420 --> 01:00:03,500
 One is you can just double-click it in the outliner, right?

1080
01:00:03,500 --> 01:00:06,620
 You can see now I can type in a name, like mug.

1081
01:00:06,620 --> 01:00:12,940
 You can also, in your 3D viewport, you can hit F2, which is the same hotkey in, like, Windows.

1082
01:00:12,940 --> 01:00:15,380
 I don't know, maybe Mac, but for renaming.

1083
01:00:15,380 --> 01:00:17,800
 And then I can just type in a name, like donut.

1084
01:00:17,800 --> 01:00:21,500
 And you can also do that in the outliner as well.

1085
01:00:21,500 --> 01:00:24,960
 You can just hit F2, and that'll also do the renaming.

1086
01:00:25,200 --> 01:00:25,600
 So, there we go.

1087
01:00:25,600 --> 01:00:27,240
 So, now we've got names that actually make sense.

1088
01:00:27,240 --> 01:00:31,000
 I don't actually need the image anymore, even though I just renamed it.

1089
01:00:31,000 --> 01:00:32,720
 So, I'm just going to delete that.

1090
01:00:32,720 --> 01:00:33,620
 All right.

1091
01:00:33,620 --> 01:00:34,820
 So, we've now just got a camera.

1092
01:00:34,820 --> 01:00:37,480
 We've got a donut, a light, and a mug.

1093
01:00:37,480 --> 01:00:38,360
 Great.

1094
01:00:38,360 --> 01:00:40,900
 So, let's make that icing.

1095
01:00:40,900 --> 01:00:43,000
 I'm going to move my mug out of the way.

1096
01:00:43,000 --> 01:00:44,340
 So, G and then X.

1097
01:00:44,340 --> 01:00:47,300
 And I'm just going to throw it to the side so we don't see it anymore.

1098
01:00:47,300 --> 01:00:50,480
 And let's focus on our donut.

1099
01:00:50,480 --> 01:00:52,620
 So, we want to make some icing.

1100
01:00:53,040 --> 01:00:58,140
 And when it comes to something as challenging as icing, and really anything, that there's

1101
01:00:58,140 --> 01:01:05,020
 so many different ways to make things in 3D, and every single method has a pro and a con.

1102
01:01:05,020 --> 01:01:07,980
 So, for example, there is a fluid simulator in Blender.

1103
01:01:07,980 --> 01:01:13,920
 It's not very good, but it exists, and it is a way you could create icing.

1104
01:01:13,920 --> 01:01:18,580
 You could actually create an object and then simulate it falling and splatting over it.

1105
01:01:18,780 --> 01:01:23,120
 The reason we're not going to do that, though, is that, one, it's incredibly complicated.

1106
01:01:23,120 --> 01:01:25,540
 There's so many things that can go wrong with a simulation.

1107
01:01:25,540 --> 01:01:27,660
 Blender simulator is not very good.

1108
01:01:27,660 --> 01:01:31,380
 And then, also, you don't have any control over the final result.

1109
01:01:31,380 --> 01:01:32,820
 You have to keep rebaking things.

1110
01:01:32,820 --> 01:01:33,560
 It's horrible.

1111
01:01:33,560 --> 01:01:37,140
 But there is another way, and that is to just do it by hand.

1112
01:01:37,140 --> 01:01:43,120
 And at first, that seems slower, but you have much more control over it, and it's actually pretty easy to do.

1113
01:01:43,120 --> 01:01:44,280
 So, I'm going to show you how to do that.

1114
01:01:44,280 --> 01:01:48,840
 And it's also a great way to introduce some more tools and things and get you familiar with different areas of Blender.

1115
01:01:48,840 --> 01:01:53,300
 So, icing follows the shape of the thing that it's on.

1116
01:01:53,300 --> 01:01:57,080
 So, really, icing is basically the shape that we've got.

1117
01:01:57,080 --> 01:01:59,140
 It's basically half of this donut.

1118
01:01:59,140 --> 01:02:02,360
 So, if we got half the donut, we're halfway there.

1119
01:02:02,360 --> 01:02:05,240
 Then we just need to add some variation to it to make it look like icing.

1120
01:02:05,240 --> 01:02:08,200
 So, what I'm going to do is duplicate my donut.

1121
01:02:08,600 --> 01:02:11,080
 So, Shift-D, that is how you duplicate.

1122
01:02:11,080 --> 01:02:16,320
 You can also just go Object, and then say Duplicate Objects there, Shift-D.

1123
01:02:16,320 --> 01:02:22,820
 And when you do this, you are in the duplicated state, and it's saying, now, where do you want to place that thing?

1124
01:02:22,820 --> 01:02:24,800
 And we don't want to place it anywhere.

1125
01:02:24,800 --> 01:02:26,420
 We want to leave it exactly where it was.

1126
01:02:26,420 --> 01:02:27,880
 And so, I'm going to hit Escape.

1127
01:02:27,880 --> 01:02:34,000
 And when you do that, it hasn't canceled the duplication, because you can see at the top there, we've now got donut and

1128
01:02:34,000 --> 01:02:35,440
 then donut.001.

1129
01:02:35,580 --> 01:02:37,420
 So, there is two objects still there.

1130
01:02:37,420 --> 01:02:41,100
 They're just exactly in the exact same spot, fighting for space.

1131
01:02:41,100 --> 01:02:46,270
 Something you actually never want to have is two objects sharing the exact same space, because then when a camera moves

1132
01:02:46,270 --> 01:02:49,260
 or something happens in an animation, you see, like, flickering.

1133
01:02:49,260 --> 01:02:50,700
 Really, really bad.

1134
01:02:50,700 --> 01:02:57,660
 Anyways, I'm going to double-click that, and I'm going to call it Icing, just so that I can quickly tell the two apart.

1135
01:02:57,660 --> 01:03:01,280
 And I want to delete the bottom half of my donut.

1136
01:03:01,680 --> 01:03:04,080
 So, I'm going to go into front view mode, like that.

1137
01:03:04,080 --> 01:03:07,380
 And I need to go into edit mode, next.

1138
01:03:07,380 --> 01:03:10,720
 And I want to select the bottom half of my donut.

