01:26:37,880 --> 01:26:38,820
 That's what we've done so far.

1588
01:26:38,820 --> 01:26:40,840
 We're going to swap to sculpting.

1589
01:26:40,840 --> 01:26:41,740
 All right.

1590
01:26:41,740 --> 01:26:47,860
 Now, when you swap to a new one of these views here, it'll, it's assuming the default view of

1591
01:26:47,860 --> 01:26:50,940
 Blender, which is like when everything was like 10 meters long or whatever.

1592
01:26:50,940 --> 01:26:52,320
 So our donut is tiny.

1593
01:26:52,320 --> 01:26:55,220
 So we have to focus on the selected object.

1594
01:26:55,220 --> 01:26:56,860
 So I'm just going to hit numpad period.

1595
01:26:56,860 --> 01:26:59,220
 That hasn't worked.

1596
01:26:59,220 --> 01:27:00,780
 Okay.

1597
01:27:00,780 --> 01:27:01,620
 Don't worry about that.

1598
01:27:01,620 --> 01:27:02,840
 All right.

1599
01:27:03,220 --> 01:27:10,880
 So instead of in sculpt mode, change to object mode, then hit numpad period or view selected.

1600
01:27:10,880 --> 01:27:13,760
 And then now jump back to sculpt mode.

1601
01:27:13,760 --> 01:27:15,060
 And now we're back to it.

1602
01:27:15,060 --> 01:27:18,240
 For some reason, I thought focus worked in, in sculpt mode.

1603
01:27:18,240 --> 01:27:18,960
 Yeah.

1604
01:27:18,960 --> 01:27:19,360
 All right.

1605
01:27:19,360 --> 01:27:22,480
 Sometimes you just teach something and then you go, oh, that never worked.

1606
01:27:22,480 --> 01:27:23,420
 What was I talking about?

1607
01:27:23,420 --> 01:27:23,900
 Anyway.

1608
01:27:23,900 --> 01:27:25,040
 Okay.

1609
01:27:25,040 --> 01:27:28,700
 In sculpt mode now, and make sure you're looking at the correct one.

1610
01:27:28,700 --> 01:27:28,880
 Okay.

1611
01:27:28,880 --> 01:27:30,680
 Not the donut, but the icing itself.

1612
01:27:31,100 --> 01:27:38,340
 If I was to now draw over this, you can see we're drawing into the mesh of the icing.

1613
01:27:38,340 --> 01:27:38,980
 Isn't that cool?

1614
01:27:38,980 --> 01:27:40,120
 All right.

1615
01:27:40,120 --> 01:27:44,480
 So what I want to do is I want to change my brush and I want to find the one brush, which

1616
01:27:44,480 --> 01:27:45,540
 is really going to help us here.

1617
01:27:45,540 --> 01:27:49,160
 And it is this one, the inflate deflate brush.

1618
01:27:49,540 --> 01:27:55,240
 Now your brush size here can be controlled by the size at the top, or there is a hotkey F.

1619
01:27:55,240 --> 01:27:59,500
 And when you hit F, you just choose with your mouse, like how big the size needs to be.

1620
01:27:59,500 --> 01:28:00,300
 Okay.

1621
01:28:00,300 --> 01:28:01,100
 So I can pull out.

1622
01:28:01,100 --> 01:28:07,940
 And then now if I just draw over that little globule there, the end piece, it just adds

1623
01:28:07,940 --> 01:28:09,680
 a little bit of inflation.

1624
01:28:11,280 --> 01:28:13,760
 And so I'm just pulling it around just slightly.

1625
01:28:13,760 --> 01:28:19,360
 I don't want to go too far, but just adding these little inflated points will really help

1626
01:28:19,360 --> 01:28:20,740
 this to look realistic.

1627
01:28:20,740 --> 01:28:24,560
 The other thing is you can actually shape some of the mesh as well.

1628
01:28:24,560 --> 01:28:28,320
 So there's another brush that I use all the time, which is the grab brush.

1629
01:28:28,860 --> 01:28:32,320
 You just essentially just pull mesh like that.

1630
01:28:32,320 --> 01:28:37,500
 So that's another easy way to just kind of move pieces around like so.

1631
01:28:37,500 --> 01:28:41,600
 You can just shape things like this, add a little bit of extra detail if you want.

1632
01:28:41,600 --> 01:28:43,120
 Yep.

1633
01:28:43,120 --> 01:28:46,360
 And all is well.

1634
01:28:46,360 --> 01:28:53,380
 So you can see I'm doing a little bit on the edge here again, because all the edges there

1635
01:28:53,380 --> 01:28:58,020
 should have a little bit of buildup of the fluid, because that's where gravity kind of makes

1636
01:28:58,020 --> 01:29:03,460
 it stall and like stop because it just kind of like runs out of like the tension will be

1637
01:29:03,460 --> 01:29:05,980
 more than the gravity that's pulling it down.

1638
01:29:05,980 --> 01:29:08,360
 You get the idea.

1639
01:29:08,360 --> 01:29:12,420
 Anyways, play with that as much as you like.

1640
01:29:12,420 --> 01:29:17,320
 But of course, we have some homework to finish off this video.

1641
01:29:17,320 --> 01:29:25,580
 So what I want you to make is this hidden object, which is a plate for your donut.

1642
01:29:26,240 --> 01:29:31,260
 So have a look at the shape here that I've got and have a think about how you would go about

1643
01:29:31,260 --> 01:29:33,580
 creating this and try to do it yourself.

1644
01:29:33,580 --> 01:29:34,980
 Just give it a stab.

1645
01:29:34,980 --> 01:29:37,740
 Even if you don't feel confident, you don't feel like you could achieve it.

1646
01:29:37,740 --> 01:29:38,880
 Just give it a go.

1647
01:29:38,880 --> 01:29:40,540
 There's a hint.

1648
01:29:40,540 --> 01:29:42,640
 We've got some modifiers on the right here.

1649
01:29:42,680 --> 01:29:48,500
 And I will also show you what the wireframe looks like, because that will probably help

1650
01:29:48,500 --> 01:29:49,780
 some people out as well.

1651
01:29:49,780 --> 01:29:50,420
 Okay.

1652
01:29:50,420 --> 01:29:52,920
 So that's a pretty big hint.

1653
01:29:52,920 --> 01:29:54,600
 But yeah, give it a go.

1654
01:29:54,600 --> 01:29:57,460
 Without any instruction from me, have a stab at that.

1655
01:29:57,460 --> 01:30:01,880
 In the next part, I'll very quickly show you how to create this.

1656
01:30:02,520 --> 01:30:05,520
 If you get completely lost, you can join me in that part.

1657
01:30:05,520 --> 01:30:07,340
 But please have a go at trying to do it yourself.

1658
01:30:07,340 --> 01:30:08,640
 Anyway, go ahead.

1659
01:30:08,640 --> 01:30:13,680
 Join me in the next part after you have tried to create this by yourself.

1660
01:30:13,680 --> 01:30:16,800
 Welcome back.

1661
01:30:16,800 --> 01:30:19,240
 How did you go making your plate?

1662
01:30:19,240 --> 01:30:21,160
 Hope you all attempted it.

1663
01:30:21,380 --> 01:30:25,200
 If you didn't, or you got stuck, don't worry, because I'm going to show you how to make it

1664
01:30:25,200 --> 01:30:26,020
 right now.

1665
01:30:26,020 --> 01:30:29,060
 So if you had no problems with it, you might want to skip forward.

1666
01:30:29,060 --> 01:30:30,160
 This might be a little boring.

1667
01:30:30,160 --> 01:30:33,780
 But otherwise, I'm going to show everyone how I would go about creating the plate.

1668
01:30:33,780 --> 01:30:36,600
 So we need to add a new object to the scene.

1669
01:30:36,600 --> 01:30:39,640
 We can go to the Add menu up there, or we can hit Shift A.

1670
01:30:39,640 --> 01:30:44,020
 And then from this list, we want to choose the object which most resembles what we want

1671
01:30:44,020 --> 01:30:44,440
 to create.

1672
01:30:44,440 --> 01:30:47,340
 If you ask me, that would be a circle.

1673
01:30:47,340 --> 01:30:48,480
 Okay.

1674
01:30:48,800 --> 01:30:52,920
 So just like before, the bottom left-hand corner, we can change some options here.

1675
01:30:52,920 --> 01:30:54,500
 The biggest one being Radius.

1676
01:30:54,500 --> 01:30:55,220
 It's too big.

1677
01:30:55,220 --> 01:30:57,440
 So I'll drop that into about there.

1678
01:30:57,440 --> 01:31:00,400
 And you will notice that you can see through it.

1679
01:31:00,400 --> 01:31:00,860
 Okay.

1680
01:31:00,860 --> 01:31:03,200
 And that is because there is no face there.

1681
01:31:03,200 --> 01:31:05,140
 This is just a string of vertices.

1682
01:31:05,140 --> 01:31:09,560
 And as I touched on briefly earlier, vertices don't show up in your render.

1683
01:31:09,560 --> 01:31:10,680
 Faces do.

1684
01:31:10,680 --> 01:31:14,420
 So we need to add a type of face underneath Fill.

1685
01:31:14,420 --> 01:31:15,860
 So there's two options.

1686
01:31:15,860 --> 01:31:17,020
 Either of them will do.

1687
01:31:17,260 --> 01:31:20,340
 Ngon is just one single face shared amongst the 32.

1688
01:31:20,340 --> 01:31:24,660
 Or the triangle fan will mean there's one point in the middle.

1689
01:31:24,660 --> 01:31:27,180
 You can actually see it in this mode.

1690
01:31:27,180 --> 01:31:27,980
 One point in the middle.

1691
01:31:27,980 --> 01:31:30,720
 And then it just creates triangles to that point.

1692
01:31:30,720 --> 01:31:36,000
 I'm going to go with Ngon because, you know, it's never failed me for this option.

1693
01:31:36,000 --> 01:31:37,860
 So I'll just use that.

1694
01:31:37,860 --> 01:31:38,760
 Okay.

1695
01:31:39,280 --> 01:31:44,780
 Now we need to add more geometry to this because we need to create some sort of a lip for the plate.

1696
01:31:44,780 --> 01:31:46,000
 You can't just have a plate.

1697
01:31:46,000 --> 01:31:48,760
 It's always got to have a rim on or that stuff slides off it, right?

1698
01:31:48,760 --> 01:31:51,580
 So into edit mode by hitting Tab.

1699
01:31:51,580 --> 01:31:55,060
 To add some geometry, I'm going to extrude.

1700
01:31:55,060 --> 01:31:56,120
 So I'm going to hit E.

1701
01:31:56,120 --> 01:31:56,620
 Okay.

1702
01:31:56,620 --> 01:32:00,100
 And in this state, it's now saying, yeah, where do you want to place it?

1703
01:32:00,100 --> 01:32:04,820
 And it's extruding it along the normal, which is that invisible line that faces out from the face in there.

1704
01:32:05,560 --> 01:32:08,520
 And I'm just going to do a single click right here.

1705
01:32:08,520 --> 01:32:14,580
 Now, before we start scaling and pulling this out, you'll notice that, obviously, that's intersecting our mesh.

1706
01:32:14,580 --> 01:32:17,920
 We need to delete that face because we don't need it.

1707
01:32:17,920 --> 01:32:20,220
 So I'm going to hit X to delete.

1708
01:32:20,220 --> 01:32:25,780
 And then what type of deletion, not edges, not vertices, obviously, because that would be, you know, the entire line.

1709
01:32:25,780 --> 01:32:29,340
 I want to delete just the single face on top.

1710
01:32:29,340 --> 01:32:30,400
 And there we go.

1711
01:32:30,400 --> 01:32:34,960
 So now I can reselect that line by holding down Alt and then left-clicking on it.

1712
01:32:35,300 --> 01:32:40,380
 And now if I want to create like a lip, which is not just straight up but a little bit out, I would just scale.

1713
01:32:40,380 --> 01:32:41,860
 So S to scale.

1714
01:32:41,860 --> 01:32:46,260
 And then just pulling my mouse out until it looks something like that.

1715
01:32:46,260 --> 01:32:47,420
 Do a click to confirm.

1716
01:32:47,420 --> 01:32:48,320
 Okay.

1717
01:32:48,320 --> 01:32:52,840
 And then I'm going to do another extrusion because I'm going to create like another bit, like a little bit of a flat

1718
01:32:52,840 --> 01:32:53,180
 area.

1719
01:32:53,180 --> 01:32:54,080
 Okay.

1720
01:32:54,080 --> 01:32:54,800
 So E.

1721
01:32:54,800 --> 01:32:57,720
 And then I don't actually want to pull it upwards.

1722
01:32:57,720 --> 01:32:59,180
 Obviously, I want to pull it outwards.

1723
01:32:59,400 --> 01:33:01,420
 So what I do is I cancel the movement.

1724
01:33:01,420 --> 01:33:06,820
 And now that I'm in that state, I'm going to hit S to pull outwards like so.

1725
01:33:06,820 --> 01:33:09,140
 By the way, I should show you as well.

1726
01:33:10,020 --> 01:33:14,080
 If after you have like extruded something, like let's say you do this, right?

1727
01:33:14,080 --> 01:33:14,440
 Oops.

1728
01:33:15,320 --> 01:33:23,640
 If you did this, if you extruded and then you like cleared its position but then accidentally clicked off it, you've

1729
01:33:23,640 --> 01:33:28,880
 now got double vertices all along this line here.

1730
01:33:29,100 --> 01:33:32,560
 And that's horrible because it will create jagged artifacts in it.

1731
01:33:32,560 --> 01:33:36,420
 So this is a really super common problem that you will experience all the time.

1732
01:33:36,420 --> 01:33:44,560
 And the way to fix it is to select your entire mesh, then hit M to merge, and then you're going to go by distance.

1733
01:33:44,560 --> 01:33:50,250
 And you'll see in the bottom there, that little thing that says removed 32 vertices, and that has cleared up that

1734
01:33:50,250 --> 01:33:50,960
 problem.

1735
01:33:50,960 --> 01:33:53,720
 So I have to show you that because people will do it.

1736
01:33:53,720 --> 01:33:54,340
 It'll happen.

1737
01:33:54,340 --> 01:33:56,160
 And that's how you fix that issue.

1738
01:33:56,160 --> 01:33:59,860
 But again, if you run into issues like this, use my Unstuck Yourself guide.

1739
01:33:59,860 --> 01:34:05,340
 Link is in the description because I did set it up so you guys can hopefully get through this tutorial.

1740
01:34:05,340 --> 01:34:06,340
 All right.

1741
01:34:06,340 --> 01:34:09,020
 So as I said, extrude, clear.

1742
01:34:09,020 --> 01:34:10,720
 It's like movement.

1743
01:34:10,720 --> 01:34:12,840
 So its placement is exactly where it started from.

1744
01:34:12,840 --> 01:34:16,180
 But then I'm going to hit S to scale outwards.

1745
01:34:16,180 --> 01:34:19,140
 So now I've got a little flat rim to my plate.

1746
01:34:19,140 --> 01:34:23,480
 And then now that it's here, I'm going to do one further extrusion upwards.

1747
01:34:23,480 --> 01:34:26,160
 And this is just obviously the design of the plate that I'm choosing.

1748
01:34:26,160 --> 01:34:27,260
 So upwards.

1749
01:34:27,260 --> 01:34:29,020
 Now you can see it's like freehand like that.

1750
01:34:29,020 --> 01:34:29,620
 I don't like it.

1751
01:34:29,620 --> 01:34:31,480
 So I'm just going to lock it on the Z axis.

1752
01:34:31,480 --> 01:34:32,840
 So just hit Z.

1753
01:34:32,840 --> 01:34:35,740
 So it's going straight up like so.

1754
01:34:35,740 --> 01:34:36,420
 Okay.

1755
01:34:36,420 --> 01:34:37,480
 Great.

1756
01:34:37,480 --> 01:34:42,040
 We've got a very jaggedy looking plate that you sometimes see in video games.

1757
01:34:42,040 --> 01:34:43,420
 You're playing Call of Duty or something.

1758
01:34:43,420 --> 01:34:46,300
 You get up close and you go like, look at that jagged old plate there.

1759
01:34:47,780 --> 01:34:53,040
 Which, by the way, is because in games, the plate is like one of a million objects and they

1760
01:34:53,040 --> 01:34:55,300
 can only put so many polys into the scene.

1761
01:34:55,300 --> 01:35:02,080
 So little things, little details on tables and things often end up a little low res.

1762
01:35:02,080 --> 01:35:04,540
 Anyways, go to your modifier stack.

1763
01:35:05,600 --> 01:35:12,420
 And the first one I want to add to kind of smooth this out is our very favorite subdivision surface

1764
01:35:12,420 --> 01:35:13,820
 right where my head is.

1765
01:35:13,820 --> 01:35:16,420
 Or you can access it from the search.

1766
01:35:16,420 --> 01:35:18,240
 Subdivision.

1767
01:35:18,240 --> 01:35:18,760
 There we go.

1768
01:35:19,280 --> 01:35:19,500
 Okay.

1769
01:35:19,500 --> 01:35:19,900
 Cool.

1770
01:35:19,900 --> 01:35:21,060
 And I'm going to go level two.

1771
01:35:21,060 --> 01:35:25,960
 And we've got that crimping effect happening to the middle of our plate there.

1772
01:35:25,960 --> 01:35:27,180
 So just like before.

1773
01:35:27,180 --> 01:35:32,100
 And actually, let's hide my donut and my icing just so that I can focus on the plate.

1774
01:35:32,100 --> 01:35:37,760
 And I'm also going to turn off the subdiv just so that I can select this single face here.

1775
01:35:37,760 --> 01:35:41,960
 Because just like before, the reason that's happening is it's like right on the edge there.

1776
01:35:41,960 --> 01:35:45,200
 It has to like convert all that into like a single point in the middle.

1777
01:35:45,200 --> 01:35:47,360
 And it's just too ugly when it starts there.

1778
01:35:47,480 --> 01:35:50,980
 So we just may have to make it start way further in the middle like that.

1779
01:35:50,980 --> 01:35:53,080
 And then when we do that, it's clear.

1780
01:35:53,080 --> 01:35:54,880
 It's practically invisible.

1781
01:35:54,880 --> 01:35:57,120
 You can only see it if you get really close.

1782
01:35:57,120 --> 01:35:59,220
 But you won't see it because it'll be under the donut and it'll be smooth.

1783
01:35:59,220 --> 01:35:59,560
 Anyway.

1784
01:35:59,560 --> 01:36:01,500
 Cool.

1785
01:36:01,500 --> 01:36:06,700
 Now I can tighten up the edges here because this is looking very, very smooth.

1786
01:36:06,700 --> 01:36:09,880
 So I showed you before how you can add a loop cut.

1787
01:36:09,880 --> 01:36:11,200
 You can do Control-R.

1788
01:36:11,200 --> 01:36:12,000
 Okay.

1789
01:36:12,000 --> 01:36:13,340
 And that'll add a loop cut in the middle.

1790
01:36:13,480 --> 01:36:21,400
 And then I just pull it out towards the edge like that to make a smooth, sorry, a sharpened point.

1791
01:36:21,400 --> 01:36:22,840
 Anyway.

1792
01:36:22,840 --> 01:36:28,940
 The other way to do it, which I didn't talk about, but you can do a crease.

1793
01:36:28,940 --> 01:36:33,640
 So if we just hold down Alt and then we select that line there, you can do a crease.

1794
01:36:33,640 --> 01:36:38,760
 If you right-click, you'll see you've got their edge crease or the hotkey Shift-E.

1795
01:36:38,760 --> 01:36:46,180
 So in this, when you do Shift-E, you'll see at the top there, you're pulling out basically a value from one to zero.

1796
01:36:46,180 --> 01:36:51,860
 And essentially what it's doing is it's like doing a fake kind of tighten on that edge there.

1797
01:36:51,860 --> 01:36:55,580
 So it's kind of like pulling it out and just making it a sharper point.

1798
01:36:55,820 --> 01:36:57,240
 It doesn't look as nice.

1799
01:36:57,240 --> 01:37:02,460
 You don't get as much of a bevel because there's no geometry being added there like you would if you were to actually

1800
01:37:02,460 --> 01:37:03,320
 do a loop cut.

1801
01:37:03,320 --> 01:37:04,760
 You get all this extra geometry.

1802
01:37:04,760 --> 01:37:12,400
 So it's not as good, but for like small things, it's, you know, it's generally fine to do something like that.

1803
01:37:12,400 --> 01:37:13,240
 All right.

1804
01:37:13,240 --> 01:37:16,380
 I'm going to do a shade smooth now because I want to see how that looks.

1805
01:37:16,380 --> 01:37:17,760
 And that's pretty good.

1806
01:37:18,260 --> 01:37:24,780
 Now, the only other thing, the exact same thing, it's weird that like three things in this small little scene have the

1807
01:37:24,780 --> 01:37:29,620
 exact same two modifiers, subserve and solidify.

1808
01:37:29,620 --> 01:37:32,600
 Anyways, generate, solidify.

1809
01:37:32,600 --> 01:37:37,720
 And just like before, we want this solidify to go first because otherwise it's doing it after the fact.

1810
01:37:37,720 --> 01:37:41,800
 And then we get this like really annoying, like hard line there.

1811
01:37:41,800 --> 01:37:44,220
 So just pull that up to the top and now it's rounded.

1812
01:37:44,220 --> 01:37:45,780
 And look at that.

1813
01:37:47,020 --> 01:37:51,600
 But, yeah, I might actually want to make this line a little bit harder.

1814
01:37:51,600 --> 01:37:53,140
 So I'll pull that in.

1815
01:37:53,140 --> 01:37:56,140
 It's still, yeah, there's just not enough geometry there.

1816
01:37:56,140 --> 01:37:58,420
 This is one of the cases where you just need more geometry.

1817
01:37:58,420 --> 01:37:59,900
 So I'm going to add a loop cut here.

1818
01:37:59,900 --> 01:38:04,160
 And I'll add another loop cut underneath it like that.

1819
01:38:04,160 --> 01:38:08,320
 And now I'm going to just select this.

1820
01:38:08,320 --> 01:38:10,000
 I'm just going to revert that edge crease.

1821
01:38:10,000 --> 01:38:13,860
 So Shift-E and then I just go like minus one like so.

1822
01:38:13,860 --> 01:38:15,880
 And now it's just the way it was before.

1823
01:38:15,880 --> 01:38:16,740
 But there you go.

1824
01:38:16,780 --> 01:38:18,320
 So now I've got a sharper edge on it.

1825
01:38:18,320 --> 01:38:21,140
 The only other thing, I might just pull up this lip here.

1826
01:38:21,140 --> 01:38:23,140
 So Alt-Select on that lip.

1827
01:38:23,140 --> 01:38:26,140
 G, Z, and I'll just pull it up a little bit.

1828
01:38:26,140 --> 01:38:28,480
 And there you go.

1829
01:38:28,480 --> 01:38:34,580
 And we would model from reference, but I actually had like a photo scan plate from Polygon, which we'll get to later.

1830
01:38:35,160 --> 01:38:38,360
 But yeah, this is just modeling from that reference that I already had.

1831
01:38:38,360 --> 01:38:39,900
 But this is the design of it.

1832
01:38:39,900 --> 01:38:40,540
 So there you go.

1833
01:38:40,540 --> 01:38:40,880
 So there you go.

1834
01:38:40,880 --> 01:38:41,760
 Great.

1835
01:38:41,760 --> 01:38:44,740
 Now let's bring back our donut and our icing.

1836
01:38:44,740 --> 01:38:51,020
 Now I saw a comment on the last video that said, you didn't make the donut lumpy like last time.

1837
01:38:51,100 --> 01:38:51,760
 It's so over.

1838
01:38:51,760 --> 01:38:52,240
 Why?

1839
01:38:52,240 --> 01:38:54,800
 Oh, ye have little faith.

1840
01:38:54,800 --> 01:38:57,200
 You think I would forget something like that?

1841
01:38:57,200 --> 01:38:59,560
 Maybe I did forget.

1842
01:39:00,660 --> 01:39:05,460
 But I'm actually glad I did because this is a great opportunity to talk about a new tool.

1843
01:39:05,460 --> 01:39:13,020
 So for those that haven't watched a previous version, before we did the icing, we made the donut lumpy, which I did by

1844
01:39:13,020 --> 01:39:14,160
 going into edit mode.

1845
01:39:14,160 --> 01:39:19,900
 And then I just selected vertices using proportional editing and like, you know, like pulled it out, right?

1846
01:39:19,900 --> 01:39:22,740
 And just kind of like maybe I shrink some parts in, this kind of thing.

1847
01:39:22,920 --> 01:39:31,440
 And the reason this is important is that this adds natural variation because, you know, you could have scientists

1848
01:39:31,440 --> 01:39:39,020
 working for a thousand years in a lab and they would never be able to create a donut as perfectly mathematically

1849
01:39:39,020 --> 01:39:41,800
 accurate as our 3D software has done.

1850
01:39:41,800 --> 01:39:43,460
 This is too perfect.

1851
01:39:43,460 --> 01:39:47,260
 There is natural variation and we need to see that for the eye to go.

1852
01:39:47,260 --> 01:39:50,820
 This is, you know, a real, you know, something realistic, right?

1853
01:39:51,000 --> 01:39:57,120
 And it's hard to do this kind of thing after you've already added the donut, sorry, the icing, because you can see it

1854
01:39:57,120 --> 01:39:59,980
 just has no bearing on the icing over the top, right?

1855
01:39:59,980 --> 01:40:04,620
 So some things in 3D are easier to do earlier than they are to do later.

1856
01:40:04,620 --> 01:40:06,460
 And this is something you just learn as you do 3D.

1857
01:40:06,460 --> 01:40:11,230
 Like you'll do so many projects where you just like you get to the end and you go like, oh, man, why didn't I do that

1858
01:40:11,230 --> 01:40:12,300
 before I did this?

1859
01:40:12,300 --> 01:40:15,340
 Now this is like I've like 3X the amount of work.

1860
01:40:15,340 --> 01:40:17,480
 And you just learn these things over time.

1861
01:40:17,900 --> 01:40:23,680
 However, there are certain tools and there is one I'm going to introduce you to in a second that enable you to make

1862
01:40:23,680 --> 01:40:25,480
 these sort of changes after.

1863
01:40:25,480 --> 01:40:32,780
 By the way, there is a way like you can select both objects like this, which that is something that you can do in

1864
01:40:32,780 --> 01:40:33,500
 London.

1865
01:40:33,500 --> 01:40:37,040
 Now you didn't used to always be able to, but you can now edit both objects together.

1866
01:40:37,040 --> 01:40:38,220
 So I could do this.

1867
01:40:38,360 --> 01:40:43,880
 It does have like a clipping issue when you try to do like shrink fatten, but you can get some like natural lumpy

1868
01:40:43,880 --> 01:40:45,460
 variations going this way.

1869
01:40:45,460 --> 01:40:47,280
 And you can do that.

1870
01:40:47,280 --> 01:40:48,140
 That's actually fine.

1871
01:40:48,140 --> 01:40:51,500
 But I do want to introduce you to a new tool that is in 5.0.

1872
01:40:52,880 --> 01:40:59,660
 And it is if you select both of your icing and your donut together, then you hit Shift A and then you go Lattice.

1873
01:40:59,660 --> 01:41:02,240
 There is a new option for Lattice Deform Selected.

1874
01:41:02,240 --> 01:41:04,940
 We always had Lattice, but it was very finicky to set up.

1875
01:41:04,940 --> 01:41:07,320
 But now you've got Lattice Deform Selected.

1876
01:41:07,440 --> 01:41:12,260
 And that adds this, what looks like a cube, like a bounding box over your object.

1877
01:41:12,260 --> 01:41:15,060
 But it's not actually going to appear in your render.

1878
01:41:15,060 --> 01:41:18,220
 All it is, is a defamation guide.

1879
01:41:18,220 --> 01:41:22,920
 So if you just select these points, you can see we are twisting the donut.

1880
01:41:22,920 --> 01:41:28,640
 But importantly, the actual donut is underneath, like this is a modifier, right?

1881
01:41:28,640 --> 01:41:31,120
 So we're not actually changing the mesh at all.

1882
01:41:31,120 --> 01:41:33,260
 It's just doing this after the fact.

1883
01:41:33,260 --> 01:41:35,660
 And then if I just deleted my Lattice, like it's gone.

1884
01:41:35,660 --> 01:41:36,560
 It's back to where it was.

1885
01:41:36,820 --> 01:41:40,200
 So this is a non-destructive workflow.

1886
01:41:40,200 --> 01:41:44,940
 These little points here, they enable me to, at any point in the future, change and deform a mesh,

1887
01:41:44,940 --> 01:41:46,700
 which is actually really useful.

1888
01:41:46,700 --> 01:41:53,140
 And it's because you're not often in the exact circumstance when you know exactly what you're going to do.

1889
01:41:53,140 --> 01:41:56,180
 And then at the end of it, like, you know the order in which to do things.

1890
01:41:56,180 --> 01:41:58,880
 Sometimes you're working with a client and they've got an idea for something.

1891
01:41:58,880 --> 01:42:00,880
 And then far later on, they're like, you know what?

1892
01:42:00,880 --> 01:42:03,880
 Let's change the proportions of the car or whatever it is.

1893
01:42:04,080 --> 01:42:07,500
 And you have to be able to, like, fix it without redoing all your mesh.

1894
01:42:07,500 --> 01:42:09,920
 So that's what a Lattice can do.

1895
01:42:09,920 --> 01:42:13,600
 Now, it comes by default with just this low-res cube over it.

1896
01:42:13,600 --> 01:42:21,460
 But if down here, you click this little data property for the Lattice, you've got resolution here.

1897
01:42:21,460 --> 01:42:24,900
 So I can increase this, I can add in some more resolution here.

1898
01:42:24,900 --> 01:42:27,380
 And unfortunately, I want to add one in the middle here.

1899
01:42:27,380 --> 01:42:33,820
 Because the first thing you notice when you look at reference of donuts is that besides the lumpy shape,

1900
01:42:33,820 --> 01:42:39,400
 the bit around the middle of the donut is often whiter and it's not as puffy.

1901
01:42:39,400 --> 01:42:43,000
 And when you look at how donuts are cooked, it's actually easy to see why.

1902
01:42:43,360 --> 01:42:49,480
 It's half submerged in the oil and then it is flipped over onto the other side and then the other side cooks.

1903
01:42:49,480 --> 01:42:50,820
 So it cooks one side and then it cooks the other.

1904
01:42:50,820 --> 01:42:59,620
 And the bits, sorry, near the surface of the oil, that's the oil is less hot, right?

1905
01:42:59,620 --> 01:43:01,840
 Because it's near the surface, so it's escaping.

1906
01:43:01,840 --> 01:43:03,060
 So that oil is less hot.

1907
01:43:03,060 --> 01:43:06,340
 The oil that's underneath it is much hotter and then it flips it over.

1908
01:43:06,340 --> 01:43:12,600
 And so, again, you always have this bit around the middle that just isn't quite cooked as well as the other stuff.

1909
01:43:12,600 --> 01:43:13,920
 So it ends up being a little bit lighter.

1910
01:43:13,920 --> 01:43:15,320
 It's also less puffy.

1911
01:43:15,320 --> 01:43:18,760
 So no donut actually comes out at that perfectly round shape.

1912
01:43:18,760 --> 01:43:22,480
 It's usually always kind of almost concave actually around the middle.

1913
01:43:22,480 --> 01:43:27,200
 So with this, I can select all of these points here like so.

1914
01:43:27,200 --> 01:43:30,000
 I have to shift select them.

1915
01:43:30,000 --> 01:43:32,840
 I can't do the alt click around it because it's not technically a mesh.

1916
01:43:33,420 --> 01:43:40,500
 And then I can just scale it in like turn off proportional editing if you've got it on and then just shrink it in like

1917
01:43:40,500 --> 01:43:41,100
 that.

1918
01:43:41,100 --> 01:43:48,200
 And that by itself will add just a little bit of realism to this because that is a detail that your eye won't notice.

1919
01:43:48,200 --> 01:43:52,500
 Everyone thinks donuts are just, you know, they're perfectly around, but they're actually not, right?

1920
01:43:52,500 --> 01:43:55,100
 They've always got this like concave kind of shape around it.

1921
01:43:55,100 --> 01:43:57,300
 So that will add some realism.

1922
01:43:57,900 --> 01:44:06,360
 But then I can add in some little areas here where one part, it got a little bit more puffy on that side.

1923
01:44:06,360 --> 01:44:08,100
 So that part's a little bit raised up.

1924
01:44:08,100 --> 01:44:16,200
 And obviously this isn't as detailed as if you had, you know, if you were manipulating the torus underneath, right?

1925
01:44:16,200 --> 01:44:18,220
 I can't do like, you know, small details.

1926
01:44:18,460 --> 01:44:22,320
 I can't, you know, sculpt in an area or do some crazy changes.

1927
01:44:22,320 --> 01:44:25,860
 But it's enough in a pinch to do something like this.

1928
01:44:25,860 --> 01:44:31,480
 I'm just going to hide my plate, which by the way, you can do it by hitting the eyedropper, which is what I've been

1929
01:44:31,480 --> 01:44:32,160
 doing up there.

1930
01:44:32,160 --> 01:44:34,400
 It's not the eyedropper, the eye icon.

1931
01:44:34,400 --> 01:44:37,560
 You can also do it by hitting H.

1932
01:44:37,740 --> 01:44:42,200
 So you select an object in the viewport, you hit H, and then you hit Alt-H to bring it back.

1933
01:44:42,200 --> 01:44:45,800
 So H or Alt-H is a good hotkey to remember.

1934
01:44:45,800 --> 01:44:51,720
 And then here, so at the bottom here, because yeah, the same thing will happen at the bottom.

1935
01:44:51,720 --> 01:44:57,860
 There'll be parts where like the donut looks a little bit like raised on the plate a little bit like this.

1936
01:44:57,860 --> 01:45:00,260
 Just around the middle here.

1937
01:45:00,260 --> 01:45:04,580
 Like that.

1938
01:45:06,500 --> 01:45:07,220
 There you go.

1939
01:45:07,220 --> 01:45:08,380
 Something like that.

1940
01:45:08,380 --> 01:45:12,760
 But this is just a small amount of defamation, right?

1941
01:45:12,760 --> 01:45:15,760
 It might seem unnecessary, but it does help.

1942
01:45:15,760 --> 01:45:21,380
 It really does help things to feel more realistic when you go to the trouble of doing some of this.

1943
01:45:21,380 --> 01:45:24,340
 And it's super, super important.

1944
01:45:24,340 --> 01:45:26,780
 But we can also now apply it.

1945
01:45:26,780 --> 01:45:30,620
 Once I know that I like everything, I don't want to have this like lattice sitting over it forever.

1946
01:45:30,620 --> 01:45:34,800
 So I can just apply that by going to apply and then selecting the donut.

1947
01:45:34,800 --> 01:45:36,180
 And I can also say apply there.

1948
01:45:36,260 --> 01:45:38,240
 And now I can just delete this lattice.

1949
01:45:38,240 --> 01:45:41,480
 And now it's like I modeled that, right?

1950
01:45:41,480 --> 01:45:43,920
 All those shapes are now like baked into it.

1951
01:45:43,920 --> 01:45:45,560
 And it looks good.

1952
01:45:45,560 --> 01:45:51,230
 As I said before, if you did want to do any more detail, you could, you know, shift, select multiple objects, go into

1953
01:45:51,230 --> 01:45:52,040
 edit mode.

1954
01:45:52,240 --> 01:45:57,960
 And then using proportional editing, you could also do some like more targeted tweaks.

1955
01:45:57,960 --> 01:46:02,080
 If you really wanted to, you know, manipulate the donut, you could do that.

1956
01:46:02,080 --> 01:46:03,800
 So there are things that you can do.

1957
01:46:03,800 --> 01:46:05,480
 It does make a difference.

1958
01:46:05,480 --> 01:46:06,440
 It is important.

1959
01:46:07,540 --> 01:46:09,260
 But, you know, it is also optional.

1960
01:46:09,260 --> 01:46:12,800
 But it's an excuse to kind of introduce you to some more tools and things in Blender.

1961
01:46:12,800 --> 01:46:18,500
 So I'm kind of glad that I didn't do it at the start because we can introduce you to that, that kind of thing.

1962
01:46:18,580 --> 01:46:19,520
 So, all right.

1963
01:46:19,520 --> 01:46:20,720
 We've got a lumpy looking donut.

1964
01:46:20,720 --> 01:46:21,760
 Good for us.

1965
01:46:21,760 --> 01:46:23,740
 Now is the exciting bit.

1966
01:46:23,740 --> 01:46:30,900
 We can talk about rendering, making this actually look good because this is gray.

1967
01:46:31,060 --> 01:46:32,260
 This is boring.

1968
01:46:32,260 --> 01:46:36,680
 This is called the 3D viewport view, okay?

1969
01:46:36,680 --> 01:46:43,790
 The reason this looks boring and flat is that this is cheaper for your computer to actually process just the shapes in

1970
01:46:43,790 --> 01:46:44,800
 like a gray.

1971
01:46:44,800 --> 01:46:45,860
 There's no shadows.

1972
01:46:45,860 --> 01:46:46,700
 There's no lighting.

1973
01:46:46,700 --> 01:46:48,980
 There's no surface interaction or anything like that.

1974
01:46:48,980 --> 01:46:50,780
 This is fast for your computer to see.

1975
01:46:50,780 --> 01:46:57,580
 To create that pretty image that looks realistic with light calculations and everything, that is called a render.

1976
01:46:57,900 --> 01:47:04,680
 And you can do a render by either hitting F12 on your keyboard or by going to render and then saying render image.

1977
01:47:04,680 --> 01:47:05,940
 Huh.

1978
01:47:05,940 --> 01:47:07,780
 And now that has taken a snapshot.

1979
01:47:07,780 --> 01:47:11,320
 The problem is, is it looks like not the view that we were looking at.

1980
01:47:11,320 --> 01:47:12,260
 So we're looking at this view.

1981
01:47:12,260 --> 01:47:13,760
 Why did we get a view up here?

1982
01:47:13,760 --> 01:47:19,300
 The reason is, is that Blender will always take the render from the camera view.

1983
01:47:19,300 --> 01:47:22,320
 So remember that thing that's like way out in the middle of nowhere we haven't touched?

1984
01:47:22,320 --> 01:47:24,820
 It's taking the view from that camera.

1985
01:47:24,900 --> 01:47:29,300
 And you can look through the camera by hitting the camera view icon over there.

1986
01:47:29,300 --> 01:47:33,980
 So now that I'm here in this view, how do we move it in close?

1987
01:47:33,980 --> 01:47:35,080
 How do we get it close to, I think?

1988
01:47:35,080 --> 01:47:41,060
 So there's, there's a number of like traditional ways to bring a camera into, like in Blender.

1989
01:47:41,060 --> 01:47:46,580
 I'll actually show you the way I first learned to do this because it's actually just like natural to me.

1990
01:47:47,020 --> 01:47:49,260
 But then I'll show you the method that actually most people prefer.

1991
01:47:49,260 --> 01:47:52,140
 So you can just, with the camera selected, you can hit G.

1992
01:47:52,140 --> 01:47:53,600
 You can move things around like that.

1993
01:47:53,600 --> 01:47:57,880
 If you want to bring it in, like just zoom to the, like straightforward.

1994
01:47:57,880 --> 01:48:02,720
 If you hit G and then middle mouse button, that will like kind of, what's it called?

1995
01:48:02,720 --> 01:48:06,180
 Dolly, dolly in and out, which can be very useful.

1996
01:48:06,720 --> 01:48:11,000
 And then if I want to like tilt it, I can like just hit rotate like that.

1997
01:48:11,000 --> 01:48:13,420
 Or I can rotate along the Z axes, right?

1998
01:48:13,420 --> 01:48:15,180
 Because I know how to like turn the camera.

1999
01:48:15,180 --> 01:48:20,020
 So that was how I learned to use the camera and how a lot of people still like to do it.

2000
01:48:20,020 --> 01:48:25,820
 But a lot of new people prefer this because now in 5.0, it's actually just a button right here.

2001
01:48:25,940 --> 01:48:33,740
 If you click this button, now I can use the same controls that I used before to move the camera.

2002
01:48:33,740 --> 01:48:38,720
 Because if you don't have that checked, the moment you just like hit your middle mouse to like orbit somewhere else, it

2003
01:48:38,720 --> 01:48:40,500
 leaves the camera behind, okay?

2004
01:48:40,500 --> 01:48:46,780
 But when you have, when you're looking through the camera and you have this checked, now when I orbit around and I zoom

2005
01:48:46,780 --> 01:48:49,960
 and I do all the same controls as before, shift middle mouse,

2006
01:48:49,960 --> 01:48:56,660
 I can position the camera exactly where I want it, get it right there without having to worry.

2007
01:48:56,660 --> 01:49:01,760
 You just have to remember, don't then immediately orbit like you're going to go do something because the camera is

2008
01:49:01,760 --> 01:49:03,220
 still locked to your view.

2009
01:49:03,220 --> 01:49:08,200
 So you have to make sure you uncheck it when you're done and then the camera is locked to that position there.

2010
01:49:08,200 --> 01:49:11,380
 And by the way, this is like a small thing.

2011
01:49:11,380 --> 01:49:17,510
 But when you're working with like small objects, you often have to like change because blend is like designed for like

2012
01:49:17,510 --> 01:49:19,900
 environment, like big types of scenes.

2013
01:49:20,560 --> 01:49:22,080
 This, this camera is just too big.

2014
01:49:22,080 --> 01:49:27,620
 So if you go to the camera settings, click the camera thing, then go to viewport display.

2015
01:49:27,620 --> 01:49:29,760
 I just changed the display of this.

2016
01:49:29,760 --> 01:49:32,600
 This has no bearing on the actual like final render.

2017
01:49:32,600 --> 01:49:36,480
 This is just how it displays in your viewport so that it's not so gigantic.

2018
01:49:36,480 --> 01:49:37,600
 It's taking over everything.

2019
01:49:37,600 --> 01:49:38,360
 All right.

2020
01:49:38,360 --> 01:49:41,660
 The other way you can quickly look through the camera is numpad zero.

2021
01:49:41,660 --> 01:49:43,740
 So numpad zero will take you in and out.

2022
01:49:43,740 --> 01:49:44,460
 All right.

2023
01:49:44,460 --> 01:49:47,800
 But now that I'm there, I can hit F12 and look at that.

2024
01:49:47,800 --> 01:49:48,780
 That is a different look.

2025
01:49:48,820 --> 01:49:52,000
 It's still gray because the default materials are gray.

2026
01:49:52,000 --> 01:49:54,780
 But you can see there is shadow there.

2027
01:49:54,780 --> 01:49:56,640
 But check this out.

2028
01:49:56,640 --> 01:49:57,580
 We can do even more.

2029
01:49:57,580 --> 01:50:03,740
 So besides just shadow, like you might notice looking at that, by the way, you can bring back a render by hitting F11.

2030
01:50:03,740 --> 01:50:09,720
 I should also mention, by the way, the first time you do a render, I think the first time I like if you've just opened

2031
01:50:09,720 --> 01:50:15,440
 Blender for the first time and you hit render, it'll have to do a bunch of like shader compilations and stuff like that.

2032
01:50:15,640 --> 01:50:17,160
 It should only need to do that once.

2033
01:50:17,160 --> 01:50:19,680
 It shouldn't have to do it every time you open Blender.

2034
01:50:19,680 --> 01:50:23,320
 We're using EV, which is a rendering engine we'll talk about soon.

2035
01:50:24,080 --> 01:50:29,190
 But this, there is some of that every time you, you run it like to, when you create a new shader, it has to like

2036
01:50:29,190 --> 01:50:30,500
 develop those things.

2037
01:50:30,500 --> 01:50:35,640
 But anyways, it's the first time it should be slow, but it should be quicker for every render after that.

2038
01:50:35,960 --> 01:50:40,800
 Okay, so there's shadow here, but you'll notice like in here, there's no, there's no detail.

2039
01:50:40,800 --> 01:50:44,760
 Whereas in the real world, you would see like bounce lighting from the end of the plate there.

2040
01:50:44,760 --> 01:50:46,560
 You would see like some crevice information.

2041
01:50:46,560 --> 01:50:48,360
 There would be, it'd be darker and things.

2042
01:50:48,360 --> 01:50:52,080
 And that is because by default, there's an option that is turned off.

2043
01:50:52,080 --> 01:50:58,250
 So if you go to your render properties at the top there, this has all the information just pertaining to how something

2044
01:50:58,250 --> 01:50:59,780
 is going to be rendered.

2045
01:51:00,280 --> 01:51:05,020
 And by default, ray tracing is turned off, which I think is a mistake.

2046
01:51:05,020 --> 01:51:11,850
 I think they should change it to default on because so many people don't like EV, this rendering engine, because they

2047
01:51:11,850 --> 01:51:14,100
 think it just produces fake looking images.

2048
01:51:14,100 --> 01:51:17,760
 But when you turn on ray tracing, look at how much better that looks.

2049
01:51:17,760 --> 01:51:21,680
 By the way, if you want to compare renders, you can change to a new slot.

2050
01:51:21,680 --> 01:51:25,140
 And then you can do a render, another render.

2051
01:51:25,140 --> 01:51:29,620
 And then you've got the previous render and then the new render in slot two.

2052
01:51:29,620 --> 01:51:32,600
 And then you can flip back by hitting J on your keyboard.

2053
01:51:32,600 --> 01:51:36,280
 And that's a way you can like quickly compare settings, which is very useful.

2054
01:51:36,280 --> 01:51:42,020
 But you can see with ray tracing turned on, this is off, turned on, we get bounce lighting.

2055
01:51:42,020 --> 01:51:44,600
 So there's some light that's hitting the plate there and it's bouncing up.

2056
01:51:44,600 --> 01:51:46,740
 We've got like more information in the crevices.

2057
01:51:46,740 --> 01:51:50,620
 And it just looks a lot more realistic than it did before.

2058
01:51:50,620 --> 01:51:54,200
 You also have reflections when I show you some material properties.

2059
01:51:54,200 --> 01:51:59,080
 So that is something you should have on all the time when you're using EV.

2060
01:51:59,460 --> 01:52:02,420
 By the way, there is another rendering engine called Cycles.

2061
01:52:02,420 --> 01:52:04,180
 And it is much more realistic.

2062
01:52:04,180 --> 01:52:06,660
 However, it is a lot more slower.

2063
01:52:06,660 --> 01:52:12,560
 It's honestly, I'm going to show you at the end a comparison between Cycles and EV.

2064
01:52:12,560 --> 01:52:15,900
 But it's actually negligible if you know the right settings for EV.

2065
01:52:16,000 --> 01:52:21,970
 So I'm going to keep it on EV for the rest, which is actually good because EV is actually pretty powerful at doing most

2066
01:52:21,970 --> 01:52:22,620
 things.

2067
01:52:23,280 --> 01:52:25,660
 It's just, it's a game engine style.

2068
01:52:25,660 --> 01:52:31,440
 So it's a rasterized rendering engine, whereas Cycles is a path trace rendering engine.

2069
01:52:31,440 --> 01:52:36,440
 Much more computationally heavy, whereas EV is much more faster.

2070
01:52:36,440 --> 01:52:37,460
 So we're going to keep it on EV.

2071
01:52:37,460 --> 01:52:39,380
 And I'm going to show you how to get really great results with it.

2072
01:52:39,380 --> 01:52:41,520
 And then at the end, we're going to flip it to Cycles.

2073
01:52:41,680 --> 01:52:44,400
 So if you want to see that final result, you can get it.

2074
01:52:44,400 --> 01:52:46,900
 But we're not going to start there because it's just not necessary.

2075
01:52:46,900 --> 01:52:49,200
 And I don't think most people will actually need it.

2076
01:52:49,200 --> 01:52:50,880
 Okay, so I'm going to talk about some materials.

2077
01:52:50,880 --> 01:52:56,460
 But first, you'll notice like it's kind of annoying to have to like render something, like go to render it every time

2078
01:52:56,460 --> 01:52:58,300
 you want to see an image like that.

2079
01:52:58,460 --> 01:53:04,600
 And thankfully, you don't have to because in the top view here, these little icons, there is a mode here called render

2080
01:53:04,600 --> 01:53:05,420
 view mode.

2081
01:53:05,420 --> 01:53:08,800
 And this puts the render in real time.

2082
01:53:08,800 --> 01:53:10,060
 Look at that.

2083
01:53:10,060 --> 01:53:13,800
 I don't have to hit the render to wait to see my result.

2084
01:53:13,800 --> 01:53:15,840
 I can see it in my viewport.

2085
01:53:15,840 --> 01:53:23,200
 So it used to be that like rendering engines were so slow that really to do a render was like a big task.

2086
01:53:23,200 --> 01:53:25,780
 You hit render and it would take hours to process something.

2087
01:53:25,780 --> 01:53:27,720
 And it didn't look anything as good as this, right?

2088
01:53:28,100 --> 01:53:34,750
 But now rendering is like and computers are so fast that we can just have rendering running constantly whilst we are

2089
01:53:34,750 --> 01:53:35,680
 working.

2090
01:53:35,680 --> 01:53:37,900
 And it's usually not computationally heavy.

2091
01:53:37,900 --> 01:53:41,680
 So this is, yeah, this is Eevee, which is the much faster one.

2092
01:53:41,680 --> 01:53:44,220
 Cycles would be a little slower, but still.

2093
01:53:44,220 --> 01:53:47,840
 Anyways, now that we're in render view mode, let's talk about materials.

2094
01:53:47,840 --> 01:53:51,320
 So let's start with the like most different material.

2095
01:53:51,320 --> 01:53:52,180
 Let's go with the icing.

2096
01:53:52,180 --> 01:53:56,900
 So looking at our icing here, if I want to make this look pink and change its overall appearance,

2097
01:53:57,100 --> 01:54:05,570
 if I go down to my material panel down here or material properties and then I can just click on new material and now I've

2098
01:54:05,570 --> 01:54:08,520
 got a bunch of different options here.

2099
01:54:08,680 --> 01:54:16,940
 And these all do different things for the surface appearance, like metallic will make it look like metal, right?

2100
01:54:16,940 --> 01:54:20,400
 Roughness will change like how sharp the reflection looks.

2101
01:54:20,400 --> 01:54:24,780
 The base color, this is like the main one you want to change for almost every object.

2102
01:54:24,780 --> 01:54:27,120
 This is just the base color of the object.

2103
01:54:27,220 --> 01:54:30,820
 So obviously, I can make that look pink, like that.

2104
01:54:30,820 --> 01:54:33,240
 Let's pull in here a little bit closer, focus on that.

2105
01:54:33,240 --> 01:54:35,860
 So I can make it look nice and pink.

2106
01:54:35,860 --> 01:54:39,600
 The other thing is, is it's looking like way too diffused.

2107
01:54:39,600 --> 01:54:41,660
 It's almost like a chalk looking icing.

2108
01:54:41,660 --> 01:54:44,880
 But if I bring this back, I can make this look more shiny.

2109
01:54:44,880 --> 01:54:48,120
 I don't want to go like too far, because then it's like mirror reflection.

2110
01:54:48,580 --> 01:54:52,440
 I want to go like, you know, somewhere around 0.2.

2111
01:54:52,440 --> 01:54:56,000
 The great thing is, is we can really change these values at any point in the future.

2112
01:54:56,340 --> 01:55:01,830
 So when we add in other objects, and there's different lighting setups, some of the settings can look a little off at

2113
01:55:01,830 --> 01:55:02,700
 that point.

2114
01:55:02,700 --> 01:55:07,940
 So it's very, very common to like, once you've set up all your lighting and everything to like, re-evaluate your

2115
01:55:07,940 --> 01:55:08,740
 materials.

2116
01:55:08,740 --> 01:55:10,480
 But it's completely non-destructive.

2117
01:55:10,480 --> 01:55:11,960
 I can change these at any time.

2118
01:55:11,960 --> 01:55:14,940
 And now I could create some very simple materials.

2119
01:55:14,940 --> 01:55:16,800
 Like I could say, let's make this look black.

2120
01:55:16,800 --> 01:55:20,940
 Make it like a black, shiny, glossy ceramic mug.

2121
01:55:20,940 --> 01:55:24,360
 So by the way, you'll notice I'm not going all the way to black.

2122
01:55:24,660 --> 01:55:27,480
 There's a kind of a unspoken, well, it's not unspoken.

2123
01:55:27,480 --> 01:55:28,240
 People speak it.

2124
01:55:28,240 --> 01:55:33,420
 You shouldn't go fully black or fully white.

2125
01:55:33,420 --> 01:55:35,520
 Or like, same with like colors.

2126
01:55:35,520 --> 01:55:39,920
 You shouldn't go like 100% saturation on a color, because things start to clip.

2127
01:55:39,920 --> 01:55:44,280
 It's maybe a little outdated, as like now color management is getting so good.

2128
01:55:44,280 --> 01:55:48,360
 It knows actually how to handle saturated stuff and like dark things.

2129
01:55:48,360 --> 01:55:50,820
 But it's still like a general rule.

2130
01:55:50,820 --> 01:55:53,700
 You never want to go like fully black.

2131
01:55:53,940 --> 01:55:58,060
 Because things are never truly, like the real black is Vantablack, right?

2132
01:55:58,060 --> 01:56:04,100
 Which is like just this material, which is like scientifically created to like emit no reflection.

2133
01:56:04,100 --> 01:56:06,020
 Simplifying it, but yeah.

2134
01:56:06,020 --> 01:56:11,260
 So like things will, even a black mug that looks really black to you is probably not entirely black.

2135
01:56:11,260 --> 01:56:13,760
 So it's still, there's going to be a little bit of lightness to it.

2136
01:56:13,760 --> 01:56:15,140
 So I'm just adding that in there.

2137
01:56:15,740 --> 01:56:20,870
 The roughness, this is where I can make this, if I wanted this to look like a, you know, shiny ceramic mug, I could do

2138
01:56:20,870 --> 01:56:21,680
 something like that.

2139
01:56:21,680 --> 01:56:23,280
 These are just placeholders, by the way.

2140
01:56:23,280 --> 01:56:26,940
 I'm just using this as an example of how to create very simple materials.

2141
01:56:27,640 --> 01:56:33,760
 The plate here, let's go, yeah, make it just look a little bit shinier than by itself.

2142
01:56:33,760 --> 01:56:40,800
 And then the donut, let's go give it a, you know, brownish, lightish color like that.

2143
01:56:40,800 --> 01:56:43,440
 Make it a little bit darker, like so.

2144
01:56:44,160 --> 01:56:49,100
 Another value, which is super important for something like icing and the donut.

2145
01:56:49,100 --> 01:56:53,840
 And it's really important for just two things, which is food and characters.

2146
01:56:53,840 --> 01:57:00,680
 And it is that when light hits objects like this, they are mushy, right?

2147
01:57:00,680 --> 01:57:02,180
 They're made of like organic matter.

2148
01:57:02,180 --> 01:57:08,180
 And so light actually enters the object and it kind of scatters around inside it and then it re-exits it.

2149
01:57:08,180 --> 01:57:13,270
 It's the reason why when you like look at your hand in front of a light or the sun or something, you can see the light

2150
01:57:13,270 --> 01:57:13,920
 shining through.

2151
01:57:13,980 --> 01:57:16,540
 You can even see your bones through your hand if it's a bright enough light.

2152
01:57:16,540 --> 01:57:19,260
 So it's called subsurface scattering.

2153
01:57:19,260 --> 01:57:22,860
 And that value is underneath here where it says subsurface.

2154
01:57:22,860 --> 01:57:28,900
 If you go down here, you want to turn the weight all the way up to one because this is not the intensity of it.

2155
01:57:28,900 --> 01:57:32,000
 This is just like, apparently this value is supposed to be zero or one.

2156
01:57:32,000 --> 01:57:33,180
 It shouldn't be somewhere in between.

2157
01:57:33,180 --> 01:57:39,320
 To control how much of it is the scale, which is how much, how far into the surface will the light penetrate.

2158
01:57:39,320 --> 01:57:42,880
 And then you can see here, you can increase this or decrease this.

2159
01:57:42,940 --> 01:57:44,640
 And you can see the light kind of get into it.

2160
01:57:44,640 --> 01:57:48,820
 Now, by default, it looks red, which I also think is a mistake.

2161
01:57:48,820 --> 01:57:55,180
 It's very suitable for characters because characters need red because inside a character is blood, right?

2162
01:57:55,180 --> 01:57:58,560
 But for every other thing like food, it shouldn't be red.

2163
01:58:00,100 --> 01:58:05,540
 And confusingly, and I know there's a mathematical reason for this because of like how light in the physics of things,

2164
01:58:05,540 --> 01:58:09,760
 but like different wavelengths travel at different speeds, right?

2165
01:58:09,760 --> 01:58:11,980
 Inside the surface.

2166
01:58:11,980 --> 01:58:12,860
 That's how I understand it.

2167
01:58:13,100 --> 01:58:21,020
 So they've made the color of the subsurface R, G, and B separated here.

2168
01:58:21,020 --> 01:58:27,720
 So you can see we've got red as one, then we've got green as 0.2, and then blue as 0.1.

2169
01:58:27,720 --> 01:58:32,320
 So essentially, I need to clear all those out and make them one, which here's a tip.

2170
01:58:32,380 --> 01:58:35,060
 You can drag over multiple values in Blender.

2171
01:58:35,060 --> 01:58:40,550
 So if you just click and drag down across something, and then you can just type in one and then hit enter, and it makes

2172
01:58:40,550 --> 01:58:41,560
 all of those one.

2173
01:58:42,120 --> 01:58:44,360
 And then now I can, you know, tweak this.

2174
01:58:44,360 --> 01:58:47,760
 I can make this look a little bit more milky like that.

2175
01:58:47,760 --> 01:58:49,360
 But we can, you know, we can adjust those.

2176
01:58:49,360 --> 01:58:51,100
 I'll do the same thing for my donut here.

2177
01:58:51,100 --> 01:58:55,660
 Give it some subsurface, turn that up, and then I'll set this to one.

2178
01:58:55,660 --> 01:58:58,040
 And then, yeah, let's just leave it at the default value.

2179
01:58:58,040 --> 01:59:01,280
 But you can see that that's with subsurface on.

2180
01:59:01,280 --> 01:59:02,500
 Actually, let me bring the camera in.

2181
01:59:02,500 --> 01:59:03,700
 All right.

2182
01:59:03,700 --> 01:59:07,700
 I'm going to go lock it, bring this in, and I'm going to do a before and after render.

2183
01:59:07,700 --> 01:59:09,680
 So this is how you can use these slots.

2184
01:59:09,680 --> 01:59:11,440
 So this is with subsurface on.

2185
01:59:11,560 --> 01:59:17,160
 Then I'll go to slot two, and now I'm going to select this, and then I'll turn it off, and then I'll do another render.

2186
01:59:17,160 --> 01:59:19,100
 And now hit J, back and forth.

2187
01:59:19,100 --> 01:59:22,840
 So that's the difference that subsurface can make to something.

2188
01:59:22,840 --> 01:59:25,100
 You can see here where it's, like, fully dark.

2189
01:59:25,100 --> 01:59:31,040
 It's actually now the light is passing through it, and that's what gives something a milky kind of look.

2190
01:59:31,040 --> 01:59:34,120
 And it's, as I said, it's mostly true for things like food.

2191
01:59:34,120 --> 01:59:34,620
 You can see it.

