02:30:26,290 --> 02:30:31,270
 So we can really just get by with – actually, we don't need metallic because there's definitely no metal in it.

2693
02:30:32,290 --> 02:30:33,550
 I'm going to keep the roughness.

2694
02:30:33,550 --> 02:30:36,090
 That's okay to keep because I just actually want to see that.

2695
02:30:36,090 --> 02:30:42,790
 I'm going to disconnect this, which is the subsurface amount, because I want to control that myself as well.

2696
02:30:42,790 --> 02:30:45,850
 And I'm just going to leave the roughness and the normal, and that's it.

2697
02:30:45,850 --> 02:30:47,870
 Oh, I should explain how I was disconnecting those.

2698
02:30:47,870 --> 02:30:53,050
 So if you hold down Control and then right-click drag, that is how you can disconnect.

2699
02:30:53,050 --> 02:30:56,170
 You can also just click these points and then just disconnect like that.

2700
02:30:56,170 --> 02:31:01,170
 But I prefer to Control, right-click, drag, and that'll cut it, sever the tie.

2701
02:31:01,170 --> 02:31:04,970
 And now that I'm here, I'm just going to set these values to what I had before.

2702
02:31:04,970 --> 02:31:08,730
 You know, just playing it by eye, what pink color looks good.

2703
02:31:08,730 --> 02:31:15,970
 Set my subsurface amount, radius to 1, and then I'll change this scale value until it looks nice.

2704
02:31:15,970 --> 02:31:16,530
 But there you go.

2705
02:31:16,530 --> 02:31:25,210
 I've now got my fleshy pink donut, but I now have this lovely, bubbly sugar crystals.

2706
02:31:25,830 --> 02:31:27,370
 Real glaze going on the donut.

2707
02:31:27,370 --> 02:31:29,750
 All right, now let's do another fun one.

2708
02:31:29,750 --> 02:31:31,770
 Let's do the donut itself.

2709
02:31:31,770 --> 02:31:37,710
 Now, this is an interesting one that I had my team do before we did, before I started recording.

2710
02:31:37,710 --> 02:31:43,990
 I had them go out and scan a real donut, scan it all the way around, then map it to a torus,

2711
02:31:43,990 --> 02:31:46,110
 and then just get the extracted data.

2712
02:31:46,310 --> 02:31:56,310
 And because this has been already UV unwrapped, to map the exact square, just like what we, sorry, yeah, just like what

2713
02:31:56,310 --> 02:31:58,690
 is captured for this type of map here.

2714
02:31:58,690 --> 02:32:02,050
 Now, I can just apply this, and it'll automatically do it.

2715
02:32:02,050 --> 02:32:02,610
 So let's do that.

2716
02:32:02,610 --> 02:32:03,210
 Hit apply.

2717
02:32:03,370 --> 02:32:04,210
 And that's pretty good.

2718
02:32:04,210 --> 02:32:05,350
 Like, that's nice color.

2719
02:32:05,350 --> 02:32:06,850
 We've got nice bump there.

2720
02:32:06,850 --> 02:32:11,510
 If we bring back our icing, you can see that's already pretty good, right?

2721
02:32:11,790 --> 02:32:13,870
 But there is something else that we can do.

2722
02:32:13,870 --> 02:32:22,070
 Because when I was talking about shading, you might remember I mentioned that there is two different ways of creating

2723
02:32:22,070 --> 02:32:24,290
 normal, sorry, creating bumps, right?

2724
02:32:24,330 --> 02:32:28,770
 And the normal one, the purple one, that's the one we've been using thus far.

2725
02:32:28,770 --> 02:32:33,490
 But there's another one here, which comes in disconnected, and it's the displacement map.

2726
02:32:33,490 --> 02:32:41,240
 Now, in the newer versions of the Polygon add-on, like the next version that's coming out after this, you'll be able to,

2727
02:32:41,240 --> 02:32:42,690
 in this last use box here,

2728
02:32:42,690 --> 02:32:48,190
 you'll have an option that says displacement and, sorry, displacement type.

2729
02:32:48,190 --> 02:32:49,910
 You can currently see it in cycles.

2730
02:32:49,910 --> 02:32:53,230
 So if you use that, you can change it here to displacement and bump.

2731
02:32:53,530 --> 02:32:58,830
 But because Eevee only just came with this feature to enable displacement, it's not yet there.

2732
02:32:58,830 --> 02:33:01,990
 So we would have to connect it ourselves.

2733
02:33:01,990 --> 02:33:08,160
 But essentially, all it's doing is when you change that little drop down there, if I grab all these things here, hit G

2734
02:33:08,160 --> 02:33:09,310
 to move them down.

2735
02:33:09,310 --> 02:33:13,850
 And then I sever this tie with the normal, and then I take my displacement.

2736
02:33:13,850 --> 02:33:15,870
 Don't put it in the normal input, though.

2737
02:33:15,870 --> 02:33:19,550
 Put it in the displacement output of the material here.

2738
02:33:19,850 --> 02:33:27,420
 And what this is doing now is, well, it's still a fake bump, but the other setting that it would enable is here in your

2739
02:33:27,420 --> 02:33:33,240
 material properties, right down here at the bottom, where it says displacement, it would change that to displacement

2740
02:33:33,240 --> 02:33:33,810
 and bump.

2741
02:33:34,190 --> 02:33:40,690
 And now, if I select my icing, hit H to hide it, you can see that the mesh has changed, right?

2742
02:33:40,690 --> 02:33:42,010
 This is the underlying mesh.

2743
02:33:42,010 --> 02:33:43,410
 It's now kind of expanded.

2744
02:33:43,410 --> 02:33:48,770
 And this is actually the captured data of the height, and it's being projected out of the donut.

2745
02:33:49,090 --> 02:33:53,750
 But it can only use the actual data, which is on the mesh, okay?

2746
02:33:53,750 --> 02:33:59,130
 So it currently looks kind of, like, chunky, because there's not enough information.

2747
02:33:59,130 --> 02:34:04,030
 So if you go to your Modify panel here, and then what would we add to add more geometry?

2748
02:34:04,030 --> 02:34:07,790
 We would add in another subdivision surface.

2749
02:34:08,390 --> 02:34:14,990
 And now when we do that, if I increase this, you can see if I increase it two, three, four times in the viewport, you

2750
02:34:14,990 --> 02:34:18,190
 can see I get a lot more data come through, okay?

2751
02:34:18,190 --> 02:34:19,790
 I think three is about it.

2752
02:34:19,790 --> 02:34:23,690
 I'm just going to, I'll set render to three or four.

2753
02:34:23,690 --> 02:34:24,610
 Yeah, I'll set it to three.

2754
02:34:24,610 --> 02:34:25,350
 That should be fine.

2755
02:34:26,270 --> 02:34:28,010
 It comes in a little bit projected.

2756
02:34:28,010 --> 02:34:32,050
 If you want to change the scale of it, you can also do that here.

2757
02:34:32,050 --> 02:34:35,270
 I could change the scale from 0.2 to about a 0.15.

2758
02:34:35,270 --> 02:34:38,230
 Also, you should see how it looks with your icing.

2759
02:34:38,230 --> 02:34:39,450
 So I'm going to hit Alt-H.

2760
02:34:39,450 --> 02:34:42,110
 You can see it's actually, yeah, clipping through it.

2761
02:34:42,110 --> 02:34:49,710
 So I'll select my donut again, and I'll change this scale here to about a 0.12, like that.

2762
02:34:49,710 --> 02:34:53,370
 And then, of course, the other value that this is missing is the subsurface scattering,

2763
02:34:53,370 --> 02:34:57,650
 because, again, we overrid the placeholder stuff that we had there before.

2764
02:34:57,650 --> 02:35:01,810
 So in here, I'm going to sever this connection, and then I'm going to set this to one,

2765
02:35:01,810 --> 02:35:04,170
 and then I'll just make this, you know, whatever.

2766
02:35:04,170 --> 02:35:06,930
 And look, you can make this look, like, so buttery nice.

2767
02:35:06,930 --> 02:35:10,710
 Like, look at how soft and fluffy this donut looks.

2768
02:35:10,710 --> 02:35:13,150
 How cool is that?

2769
02:35:13,150 --> 02:35:14,850
 Like that.

2770
02:35:14,850 --> 02:35:16,210
 Bring it back.

2771
02:35:16,210 --> 02:35:16,630
 Look at that.

2772
02:35:16,630 --> 02:35:18,990
 We've got real icing with the real crystals.

2773
02:35:19,430 --> 02:35:24,190
 We've got real donut that has been captured and projected onto our torus.

2774
02:35:24,190 --> 02:35:26,830
 And that's just two materials, which is really cool.

2775
02:35:26,830 --> 02:35:32,470
 The final object to texture is our mug, and I'm going to do that in the next part,

2776
02:35:32,470 --> 02:35:38,210
 because we need to go much deeper into UV unwrapping and show how to unwrap a custom object.

2777
02:35:38,210 --> 02:35:40,350
 So join me in the next video.

2778
02:35:40,350 --> 02:35:47,190
 The time has come to UV unwrap and texture our mug.

2779
02:35:47,190 --> 02:35:51,490
 So we did a little bit with our icing, our donut, and our plate.

2780
02:35:51,490 --> 02:35:53,190
 We got most of that for free.

2781
02:35:53,190 --> 02:35:55,290
 Those were very simple objects.

2782
02:35:55,290 --> 02:35:58,290
 This is a little more complex, so we're going to have some fun with it.

2783
02:35:58,810 --> 02:36:03,050
 So first things first, we need to apply a texture to it so that we can see the UV unwrapping

2784
02:36:03,050 --> 02:36:04,270
 and see that it's working well.

2785
02:36:04,270 --> 02:36:09,810
 So I'm going to hit N to bring up my properties, and I'm using the polygon add-on,

2786
02:36:09,810 --> 02:36:12,370
 and I'm going to apply the ceramic texture that we're going to use.

2787
02:36:12,370 --> 02:36:16,770
 So the free one that we're using for this tutorial, you can find by typing in donut5.

2788
02:36:16,770 --> 02:36:19,510
 That'll bring up all the ones we're going to use in this tutorial.

2789
02:36:19,510 --> 02:36:24,290
 And the one we're using is this one, this rustic gold ceramic texture.

2790
02:36:24,650 --> 02:36:27,310
 So I've already downloaded it, so you'll see download.

2791
02:36:27,310 --> 02:36:29,050
 It'll look like download, like that.

2792
02:36:29,050 --> 02:36:33,250
 And then with the mug selected, you want to hit apply.

2793
02:36:33,250 --> 02:36:36,050
 If the mug isn't selected like that, you'll see import,

2794
02:36:36,050 --> 02:36:41,090
 which would just like load it in as a like fake user material that you could reference.

2795
02:36:41,090 --> 02:36:43,550
 But anyways, so select the mug and then say apply.

2796
02:36:43,550 --> 02:36:46,010
 And now we can close that, and let's take a look.

2797
02:36:46,010 --> 02:36:49,510
 Now you might be surprised at how well that came in.

2798
02:36:49,510 --> 02:36:54,310
 Like the handle is no good, but the rest of it is semi-okay, right?

2799
02:36:54,650 --> 02:36:59,890
 There's some stretching on the rim and the base, but like why did that come in okay?

2800
02:36:59,890 --> 02:37:03,210
 So if you go to the UV editor here, you can see we've got some islands,

2801
02:37:03,210 --> 02:37:08,350
 and this matches exactly what you would expect if you created a cylinder.

2802
02:37:08,350 --> 02:37:12,250
 So when we modeled this mug, you remember we started with a cylinder,

2803
02:37:12,250 --> 02:37:16,890
 and that's a primitive object, and Blender automatically UV unwraps the primitive objects for us.

2804
02:37:16,890 --> 02:37:21,110
 So everything that is still a cylinder form is okay,

2805
02:37:21,110 --> 02:37:23,990
 but everything else that we created from scratch, like this handle here,

2806
02:37:23,990 --> 02:37:25,710
 it has no idea what to do with it.

2807
02:37:25,710 --> 02:37:27,230
 So it's all stretched and all screwed up.

2808
02:37:27,230 --> 02:37:30,910
 So basically we have to start from scratch and do this again.

2809
02:37:30,910 --> 02:37:31,350
 But that's okay.

2810
02:37:31,350 --> 02:37:34,290
 That's the point of this tutorial to learn how to do UV unwrapping.

2811
02:37:34,290 --> 02:37:36,750
 So as I explained with the icing,

2812
02:37:36,750 --> 02:37:43,650
 the best way to think about UV unwrapping is to imagine that this object was made of paper,

2813
02:37:43,650 --> 02:37:49,010
 and we have to lay it flat onto a 2D surface, which is what this is over here.

2814
02:37:49,010 --> 02:37:51,070
 This is the 3D object.

2815
02:37:51,070 --> 02:37:53,470
 This is the flat surface it's being laid on.

2816
02:37:53,470 --> 02:37:57,490
 So it's like we are turning this into paper, and we have to lay it flat.

2817
02:37:57,490 --> 02:38:03,150
 Where would you put the cuts on this object in order to make it flat there?

2818
02:38:03,750 --> 02:38:07,170
 And so the most obvious place you could think of, if you're looking at doing this,

2819
02:38:07,170 --> 02:38:11,150
 is like I know for sure this handle has to be separated from the rest of it

2820
02:38:11,150 --> 02:38:13,070
 in order to start to lay it flat.

2821
02:38:13,070 --> 02:38:18,150
 By the way, before we start, it would probably help with your mug selected.

2822
02:38:18,150 --> 02:38:22,610
 In the modifiers, you've got this little box there that says on cage.

2823
02:38:22,610 --> 02:38:27,470
 So if it's turned off, it'll show you like the unsubdivided result around it,

2824
02:38:27,470 --> 02:38:29,870
 but then like parts of it get hidden by the mesh.

2825
02:38:29,870 --> 02:38:33,930
 So I usually turn it on just so that I can see it, and like all the mesh is visible.

2826
02:38:33,930 --> 02:38:36,190
 It just makes UV unwrapping it a little bit easier.

2827
02:38:36,190 --> 02:38:38,010
 Okay, so the handle needs to be separated.

2828
02:38:38,010 --> 02:38:40,430
 So right here is an incision point, right?

2829
02:38:40,430 --> 02:38:45,030
 So I'm going to select these vertices here by holding down shift and just shift selecting.

2830
02:38:45,030 --> 02:38:49,330
 Then I'm going to hit control E, and then select mark seam.

2831
02:38:49,330 --> 02:38:52,950
 Now, Blender won't have updated yet, so this hasn't changed over here.

2832
02:38:53,310 --> 02:38:58,810
 So in order to see that, we would first have to select the whole mesh, then go U, and then select unwrap.

2833
02:38:58,810 --> 02:39:01,770
 Or the method that I prefer to do, and I would suggest you do this too,

2834
02:39:01,770 --> 02:39:05,870
 in your 3D view over here, there's a little options drop down at the top there.

2835
02:39:05,870 --> 02:39:08,770
 If you click that, and then select live unwrap.

2836
02:39:08,770 --> 02:39:14,590
 Now, whenever you do a seam, so let's just do another seam here, mark seam on the same spot,

2837
02:39:14,590 --> 02:39:17,590
 it will automatically do that UV unwrap for you.

2838
02:39:17,610 --> 02:39:18,370
 So that's really helpful.

2839
02:39:18,370 --> 02:39:20,310
 Now, it looks worse.

2840
02:39:20,310 --> 02:39:23,810
 You have to go backwards before you can go forwards, but that's okay.

2841
02:39:23,810 --> 02:39:27,870
 So we've only got one incision, so let's do another one, the other half of the handle.

2842
02:39:27,870 --> 02:39:33,870
 So down here, I'm going to select these points, like so, and then hit control E, and then mark seam.

2843
02:39:33,870 --> 02:39:36,310
 Okay, so now it has changed.

2844
02:39:36,310 --> 02:39:38,010
 So we've got the handle here.

2845
02:39:38,010 --> 02:39:42,190
 Now, if you want to select just the handle, by the way, in the 3D viewport,

2846
02:39:42,190 --> 02:39:48,530
 if you just mouse over that part of the motion, of the motion, the mesh, and then hit L,

2847
02:39:48,530 --> 02:39:54,050
 then in this little last use box here, if you select seam,

2848
02:39:54,050 --> 02:39:57,490
 it'll now just select the parts of the mesh that match that seam.

2849
02:39:57,490 --> 02:40:00,390
 By the way, I should also be in edge select mode.

2850
02:40:00,390 --> 02:40:03,770
 That would definitely be helpful because we're placing seams on edges.

2851
02:40:03,770 --> 02:40:08,290
 But yeah, if I do that now, that selection, you can clearly see that this is the part of the mesh,

2852
02:40:08,290 --> 02:40:10,110
 which is this handle over here.

2853
02:40:10,310 --> 02:40:12,030
 So it's been separated, okay?

2854
02:40:12,030 --> 02:40:13,610
 So we've got like two parts of the mesh.

2855
02:40:13,610 --> 02:40:17,510
 We've got all of them, this part of the mug, which is this mess over here.

2856
02:40:17,510 --> 02:40:20,330
 And then we've got this handle here, which is this part over here.

2857
02:40:20,330 --> 02:40:23,810
 So this is like somewhat unwrapped.

2858
02:40:23,810 --> 02:40:28,190
 But again, if this was made of paper, to lay it flat, we would have to do another incision.

2859
02:40:28,190 --> 02:40:33,450
 So when I'm placing a scission like this, like I have to place an incision somewhere here, right?

2860
02:40:33,450 --> 02:40:36,090
 Like here, here, here, or here.

2861
02:40:37,090 --> 02:40:46,890
 So because a seam generally makes a seam, like a visible line in a texture that will often not be like lined up,

2862
02:40:46,890 --> 02:40:48,870
 that can look ugly, right?

2863
02:40:48,870 --> 02:40:50,750
 It's like, it's not a good look for a model.

2864
02:40:50,750 --> 02:40:54,270
 So often when you're placing a seam, you put it in a place that is hidden.

2865
02:40:54,270 --> 02:40:59,310
 So, I mean, it would be a really good spot if we had like a line right down the middle of the mug there.

2866
02:40:59,430 --> 02:41:02,010
 But next best thing, I'll place it just to the side there.

2867
02:41:02,010 --> 02:41:04,230
 So holding down Alt, select that edge.

2868
02:41:04,230 --> 02:41:06,770
 Now I'm going to hit Control-E and then say seam.

2869
02:41:06,770 --> 02:41:08,850
 And now, look at that.

2870
02:41:08,850 --> 02:41:13,510
 This now has been pretty well UV unwrapped, okay?

2871
02:41:13,510 --> 02:41:15,670
 That section there.

2872
02:41:15,670 --> 02:41:16,510
 That's pretty good.

2873
02:41:16,510 --> 02:41:19,810
 So now we just have to do the same for the rest of the mug here.

2874
02:41:19,810 --> 02:41:21,250
 And this is pretty easy to do.

2875
02:41:21,710 --> 02:41:27,630
 So, I mean, you can tell like one place we would have to put a cut would be like the base of it,

2876
02:41:27,630 --> 02:41:30,570
 like right here inside of our mug.

2877
02:41:30,570 --> 02:41:33,270
 So I'm going to hold down Alt just on this face at the bottom there.

2878
02:41:33,270 --> 02:41:39,250
 And I'm going to hit Control-E and I'm going to say seam just to separate that single circle there out by itself.

2879
02:41:39,910 --> 02:41:47,150
 Now, I need to put one cut that goes right down like here, like this part of a mesh.

2880
02:41:47,150 --> 02:41:48,570
 Like I need to do a cut right there.

2881
02:41:48,570 --> 02:41:52,310
 But again, that can create like a visible like line.

2882
02:41:52,310 --> 02:41:56,530
 So I want to try to hide it by putting it on like close to this handle here.

2883
02:41:56,530 --> 02:42:01,490
 Because also there are other seams here and it will just kind of help to just have it on one part of the mesh.

2884
02:42:01,490 --> 02:42:05,670
 Because like you could like move it and hide it by the camera and stuff like that.

2885
02:42:05,670 --> 02:42:07,230
 So it just helps to put it all in one place.

2886
02:42:07,410 --> 02:42:11,090
 So holding down Alt here, so Alt, click on that.

2887
02:42:11,090 --> 02:42:12,830
 So it goes all the way down to there.

2888
02:42:12,830 --> 02:42:15,150
 And then it kind of stops here on the handle there.

2889
02:42:15,150 --> 02:42:17,290
 So I'm going to select this line, just holding down Shift.

2890
02:42:17,290 --> 02:42:19,350
 And then I also want to do it right there.

2891
02:42:19,350 --> 02:42:21,830
 So I'm holding down Alt on that.

2892
02:42:21,830 --> 02:42:25,190
 And now I'm going to hit Control-E and then say Mark Seam.

2893
02:42:25,190 --> 02:42:29,030
 Okay, so now that's looking a lot more released, right?

2894
02:42:29,030 --> 02:42:30,730
 There's less tension in there.

2895
02:42:30,730 --> 02:42:34,150
 But there is still this like pinching effect happening at the bottom there.

2896
02:42:34,150 --> 02:42:37,310
 So just like in the middle there, when we selected that and separated it,

2897
02:42:37,410 --> 02:42:41,910
 I want to select this part of the mesh, which is underneath the mug and I want to unwrap that.

2898
02:42:41,910 --> 02:42:43,470
 Oh, sorry, place the seam there.

2899
02:42:43,470 --> 02:42:46,950
 By the way, if you want to like, because we can't really get to that very easily.

2900
02:42:46,950 --> 02:42:51,970
 If you want to just see an object by itself, you can hit forward slash.

2901
02:42:51,970 --> 02:42:55,250
 So in object mode with the mug selected, forward slash.

2902
02:42:55,250 --> 02:42:57,210
 And now everything has vanished.

2903
02:42:57,850 --> 02:43:02,230
 Now, Blender doesn't do a very good job of showing you that you have, you're just looking

2904
02:43:02,230 --> 02:43:05,390
 at an isolated view, except for at the top there where it says local.

2905
02:43:05,390 --> 02:43:09,350
 So there's many times when I'm using Blender and I think, I'm like, where's the rest of

2906
02:43:09,350 --> 02:43:10,070
 my scene?

2907
