 would have like different parts of the painting, like appearing twice across it.

3136
02:54:18,630 --> 02:54:21,890
 So there's kind of like two styles to UV unwrapping.

3137
02:54:21,890 --> 02:54:25,590
 This is the simplest one, which is just like, if you've got an image that you've got off the

3138
02:54:25,590 --> 02:54:30,230
 internet or a texture or whatever, that's just being tiled across it or even not tiled.

3139
02:54:30,230 --> 02:54:35,310
 Like it doesn't matter if things are overlapping because you know, the texture is different

3140
02:54:35,310 --> 02:54:35,590
 enough.

3141
02:54:35,590 --> 02:54:39,110
 Your eye's not going to spot it, but if it's painted, yes, they do have to be separate

3142
02:54:39,110 --> 02:54:39,450
 parts.

3143
02:54:39,450 --> 02:54:45,070
 And another thing as well, if you're UV unwrapping for a game asset, it's even more

3144
02:54:45,070 --> 02:54:49,970
 complex because all the islands have to be separated, but there's also like margin because

3145
02:54:49,970 --> 02:54:54,090
 like in games, you develop like multiple versions of a mesh at different resolutions.

3146
02:54:54,090 --> 02:54:55,970
 Then there's different texture resolutions.

3147
02:54:55,970 --> 02:54:59,770
 So you have to create like big margins and it gets all like complicated.

3148
02:54:59,770 --> 02:55:01,770
 It's like a completely separate thing.

3149
02:55:01,770 --> 02:55:06,450
 So if you wanted to texture for games, you should probably look at like game texturing type

3150
02:55:06,450 --> 02:55:08,830
 tutorials if you really wanted to go into that.

3151
02:55:08,830 --> 02:55:11,290
 But anyways, the rest of it is fine.

3152
02:55:11,290 --> 02:55:15,090
 Like that part of it or this like stretching in here, if you wanted to fix that, you could.

3153
02:55:15,090 --> 02:55:18,730
 It doesn't matter because we're going to put some like coffee foam along the top there.

3154
02:55:18,730 --> 02:55:22,570
 The base of it doesn't matter because we're not going to see it.

3155
02:55:22,990 --> 02:55:28,310
 So yeah, but that's, that's also the thing to consider is like, you know, when people are

3156
02:55:28,310 --> 02:55:31,290
 like on Twitter and they're like, look at this model, look how terrible it is.

3157
02:55:31,290 --> 02:55:34,210
 It's like, yeah, it might be for a purpose like this.

3158
02:55:34,210 --> 02:55:37,650
 It's designed to be viewed from above and that's all it needs to do.

3159
02:55:37,650 --> 02:55:41,370
 Like if you've like pulled it apart and it's like, how terrible does it look?

3160
02:55:41,370 --> 02:55:44,270
 It's like, yeah, the camera was never going to go in there or under there.

3161
02:55:44,270 --> 02:55:46,430
 So that's why it doesn't matter.

3162
02:55:46,430 --> 02:55:48,250
 Anyways.

3163
02:55:48,250 --> 02:55:48,710
 All right.

3164
02:55:48,710 --> 02:55:50,590
 So the mug is pretty good.

3165
02:55:50,590 --> 02:55:54,730
 The only thing I'll do is I'm going to go to my material over here.

3166
02:55:54,730 --> 02:55:56,090
 Actually, I'm sorry.

3167
02:55:56,090 --> 02:55:59,770
 I'll go to my shading tab at the top there so that I can access the nodes here.

3168
02:55:59,770 --> 02:56:02,070
 And I'll just make a few tweaks to it.

3169
02:56:02,070 --> 02:56:06,830
 Just like before, when I mentioned these maps here, like this is going to give you the

3170
02:56:06,830 --> 02:56:12,090
 physically accurate data from the actual ceramic that we scanned, right?

3171
02:56:12,090 --> 02:56:14,210
 And it's like color calibrated and everything like that.

3172
02:56:14,210 --> 02:56:18,690
 But sometimes you want artistic control and I want this to look shinier.

3173
02:56:18,690 --> 02:56:20,170
 This is a little dull for me.

3174
02:56:20,170 --> 02:56:24,730
 So I'm going to disconnect my roughness here and then I'm just going to play with this slider.

3175
02:56:24,730 --> 02:56:26,130
 Like so.

3176
02:56:26,130 --> 02:56:27,090
 So I've still got the bump.

3177
02:56:27,090 --> 02:56:31,870
 So like most of the detail that I want is still there, but like I don't need, I don't need,

3178
02:56:31,870 --> 02:56:34,190
 I don't need to look as dull as it was.

3179
02:56:34,190 --> 02:56:35,530
 So that is pretty good.

3180
02:56:35,530 --> 02:56:36,870
 I also don't need the metallic shader.

3181
02:56:36,870 --> 02:56:38,190
 Like that's just superfluous.

3182
02:56:38,190 --> 02:56:44,430
 There's another one here, ORM, sorry, not ORM, ambient occlusion or AO.

3183
02:56:44,430 --> 02:56:47,010
 This is another one which is completely unnecessary.

3184
02:56:47,010 --> 02:56:51,550
 It's just for like crevices of information, but this is like flat ceramics.

3185
02:56:51,550 --> 02:56:52,310
 So there's no crevices.

3186
02:56:52,310 --> 02:56:55,750
 We have to provide it like standardized for all of our materials.

3187
02:56:55,750 --> 02:56:59,010
 So they're all uniform, but a lot of materials just don't need it.

3188
02:56:59,010 --> 02:57:03,550
 So in a case like this, I just don't want the AO like this map here.

3189
02:57:03,750 --> 02:57:05,870
 So I could just like disconnect that.

3190
02:57:05,870 --> 02:57:10,430
 Or actually, if you want to delete something like this and you want it to retain its connection

3191
02:57:10,430 --> 02:57:13,750
 rather than just deleting it, because then you have to manually connect it.

3192
02:57:13,750 --> 02:57:20,290
 If instead, if you hit control X, it will delete it and retain its connection.

3193
02:57:20,290 --> 02:57:21,730
 So that's just a little tip there for you.

3194
02:57:21,730 --> 02:57:23,650
 Okay, great.

3195
02:57:23,650 --> 02:57:27,090
 So now let's go back to my layout mode over here.

3196
02:57:27,170 --> 02:57:28,690
 Let's have a look at it with our lighting.

3197
02:57:28,690 --> 02:57:30,610
 And that is pretty good.

3198
02:57:30,610 --> 02:57:37,230
 Now, the only thing I will change, though, looking at this, is this concrete is mighty pale.

3199
02:57:37,230 --> 02:57:39,270
 It doesn't look very good.

3200
02:57:39,270 --> 02:57:43,750
 It doesn't look very luxurious, like a cafe, like a highfalutin.

3201
02:57:43,750 --> 02:57:44,670
 Ooh, look at me.

3202
02:57:44,670 --> 02:57:46,510
 I'm paying $9 for a coffee.

3203
02:57:46,510 --> 02:57:47,230
 Aren't I fancy?

3204
02:57:47,230 --> 02:57:48,690
 It doesn't look like that, right?

3205
02:57:48,690 --> 02:57:50,990
 It looks like dusty, like a workman floor.

3206
02:57:51,410 --> 02:57:54,330
 So we can adjust this by going to the shading tab.

3207
02:57:54,330 --> 02:57:58,550
 So this is a great excuse to go a little into the nodes, right?

3208
02:57:58,550 --> 02:58:00,490
 So this is the one that we actually manually set up.

3209
02:58:00,490 --> 02:58:01,670
 So it's, you know, it's pretty clean.

3210
02:58:01,670 --> 02:58:04,310
 We just got a base color and we've got a normal map, right?

3211
02:58:04,310 --> 02:58:09,410
 But between here and here where it connects, we can adjust the color of this.

3212
02:58:09,410 --> 02:58:15,490
 And I'm going to show you how simple you can completely transform the look of a texture.

3213
02:58:15,970 --> 02:58:22,850
 So if I hit Shift-A or just go to Add at the top there, then go to Color, and there's a bunch of different color

3214
02:58:22,850 --> 02:58:24,150
 options, like brightness and contrast.

3215
02:58:24,150 --> 02:58:25,250
 I wouldn't use that.

3216
02:58:25,250 --> 02:58:28,070
 That's like what is brightness, what is contrast.

3217
02:58:28,070 --> 02:58:29,750
 There's a whole, like, color debate and things.

3218
02:58:29,750 --> 02:58:34,510
 Color Ramp can be useful if you just want to, like, convert something to, like, your own set of colors.

3219
02:58:34,510 --> 02:58:36,530
 Hue, Saturation Value, we use that.

3220
02:58:36,530 --> 02:58:39,490
 The one I'm looking for, though, is RGB Curves.

3221
02:58:39,670 --> 02:58:45,100
 So if I drop this in here, this will give me, if you're familiar with RGB Curves from, like, Photoshop or DaVinci or

3222
02:58:45,100 --> 02:58:49,290
 anything like that, this will let you do, like, an S-Log kind of curve.

3223
02:58:49,290 --> 02:58:55,070
 So essentially, this data at the bottom here, this is your dark, dark tones.

3224
02:58:55,070 --> 02:58:56,650
 This would be your mid-tones.

3225
02:58:56,650 --> 02:58:57,730
 This would be your highlights.

3226
02:58:57,730 --> 02:59:00,010
 And this is, like, your clipped, like, highlights at the top there.

3227
02:59:00,010 --> 02:59:06,870
 But you can see if I make this look like a ramp like this, it starts to look more contrasted, right?

3228
02:59:06,870 --> 02:59:08,510
 And I pull it all the way down.

3229
02:59:08,510 --> 02:59:11,990
 And I can, like, really transform this and make it look really contrasted.

3230
02:59:11,990 --> 02:59:14,370
 And look at how different that looks already, right?

3231
02:59:14,370 --> 02:59:18,590
 From just this one little thing, you wouldn't imagine you could convert it like this.

3232
02:59:18,590 --> 02:59:20,930
 But that's a simple thing, and it's already looking good.

3233
02:59:20,930 --> 02:59:23,550
 And then I want to bring out some of the whites as well.

3234
02:59:23,550 --> 02:59:26,690
 So I can just add at the top here just a little curve that goes the other way.

3235
02:59:26,690 --> 02:59:30,150
 So it kind of exaggerates some of those whites, okay?

3236
02:59:30,150 --> 02:59:31,550
 Great.

3237
02:59:31,550 --> 02:59:32,450
 That's pretty good.

3238
02:59:32,450 --> 02:59:37,910
 But now the other thing is, is, like, because I've, like, crunched it and made it a little more contrasty,

3239
02:59:38,010 --> 02:59:42,430
 it's also brought out some of the colors, which are looking a little, like, yellowy green,

3240
02:59:42,430 --> 02:59:43,810
 which is a little unappealing.

3241
02:59:43,810 --> 02:59:47,290
 Kind of like this table would be, like, a little moldy, not great.

3242
02:59:47,590 --> 02:59:55,090
 So instead, to improve it, I can add another node, as I mentioned, I use this one a lot, hue saturation value.

3243
02:59:55,090 --> 03:00:02,350
 So if I drop this in, and I drop it in here, you could also put it before the curves or after, doesn't really matter.

3244
03:00:02,990 --> 03:00:05,490
 And then I just want to drop the saturation here.

3245
03:00:05,490 --> 03:00:06,050
 And watch this.

3246
03:00:06,050 --> 03:00:08,010
 Watch how completely transformed this becomes.

3247
03:00:08,010 --> 03:00:09,410
 Hoo-hoo.

3248
03:00:09,410 --> 03:00:10,750
 Look at that.

3249
03:00:10,750 --> 03:00:12,270
 Doesn't that look nice?

3250
03:00:12,270 --> 03:00:13,830
 I think it looks nice.

3251
03:00:13,830 --> 03:00:18,050
 It's, like, and you can turn off these nodes, by the way, like, temporarily.

3252
03:00:18,050 --> 03:00:20,390
 If you want to mute something, it's M.

3253
03:00:20,390 --> 03:00:21,430
 M for mute.

3254
03:00:21,430 --> 03:00:23,330
 And you can select that and mute it.

3255
03:00:23,330 --> 03:00:24,510
 So this is what we started with.

3256
03:00:24,510 --> 03:00:31,310
 And then just with two nodes, we converted this pale-looking concrete into this, which is crazy.

3257
03:00:31,310 --> 03:00:33,390
 Pretty cool.

3258
03:00:33,390 --> 03:00:35,790
 Anyways, so that's nice.

3259
03:00:36,190 --> 03:00:38,830
 And then, you know, you can obviously play with the roughness and stuff.

3260
03:00:38,830 --> 03:00:42,350
 And we'll get to that when we get to the lighting a little bit more.

3261
03:00:42,350 --> 03:00:45,310
 But that is looking good.

3262
03:00:45,310 --> 03:00:50,090
 Oh, the other thing, of course, we need to add is our coffee foam.

3263
03:00:50,090 --> 03:00:52,030
 And this one is super easy.

3264
03:00:52,030 --> 03:00:56,930
 And it's another chance to talk about another type of texture, which you can find on sites like Polygon.

3265
03:00:56,930 --> 03:00:59,950
 But we need to add coffee foam to this.

3266
03:00:59,950 --> 03:01:05,990
 And to do that, we need to start with an object that is going to obviously fill the top of this.

3267
03:01:06,390 --> 03:01:09,890
 And, you know, we could go in here and we could, like, add in a circle and do that kind of thing.

3268
03:01:09,890 --> 03:01:17,050
 But it's also smart to think about, like, how can I save time by reusing part of my mesh that I already have?

3269
03:01:17,050 --> 03:01:25,250
 So I already know the exact size that the foam to fit my mug because I already have a mug here with a circle in it,

3270
03:01:25,250 --> 03:01:26,030
 right?

3271
03:01:26,030 --> 03:01:26,990
 Look at this line.

3272
03:01:26,990 --> 03:01:28,810
 That is a circle.

3273
03:01:28,810 --> 03:01:33,090
 If I could just take that and make it a new object, I can use it.

3274
03:01:33,350 --> 03:01:37,830
 So to do that, I can select that line and then hit Shift-D, right?

3275
03:01:37,830 --> 03:01:38,930
 I've got my circle now.

3276
03:01:38,930 --> 03:01:41,210
 Then I want to hit Escape, okay?

3277
03:01:41,210 --> 03:01:43,990
 So it's now just brought it back to where it was.

3278
03:01:43,990 --> 03:01:45,790
 And now I'm going to hit P.

3279
03:01:45,790 --> 03:01:49,010
 P is how you separate something to a new object.

3280
03:01:49,010 --> 03:01:50,710
 So you're separating part of the mesh.

3281
03:01:50,710 --> 03:01:54,250
 So now I'm going to hit Selection like that.

3282
03:01:54,430 --> 03:01:58,850
 And now if I go over to my outliner here, we should see two mugs.

3283
03:01:58,850 --> 03:02:03,290
 So this is my original mug and then this is my mug.001, okay?

3284
03:02:03,290 --> 03:02:06,850
 So I'm going to call this my foam, like that.

3285
03:02:06,850 --> 03:02:10,390
 And now I'm going to go into edit mode.

3286
03:02:10,390 --> 03:02:13,410
 Let's put it in edit mode.

3287
03:02:14,590 --> 03:02:18,350
 All right, and I'll turn off that little view there so that I can just see.

3288
03:02:18,350 --> 03:02:19,610
 Where is it?

3289
03:02:19,610 --> 03:02:20,310
 Hold on a second.

3290
03:02:20,310 --> 03:02:22,630
 All right, okay.

3291
03:02:22,630 --> 03:02:25,830
 So you have to go into Y-frame mode in order to see it there.

3292
03:02:25,830 --> 03:02:28,170
 But yeah, that is my circle.

3293
03:02:28,170 --> 03:02:31,390
 And actually, yeah, I should just be in my layout mode, shouldn't I?

3294
03:02:31,390 --> 03:02:31,970
 Not in shading.

3295
03:02:31,970 --> 03:02:32,770
 I should be in layout.

3296
03:02:32,770 --> 03:02:33,810
 So I'm going to go here.

3297
03:02:33,810 --> 03:02:35,110
 Let's do the same thing.

3298
03:02:35,110 --> 03:02:36,210
 Y-frame mode.

3299
03:02:36,210 --> 03:02:36,530
 Okay.

3300
03:02:37,250 --> 03:02:40,990
 So this circle obviously isn't being rendered because there's no face on it.

3301
03:02:40,990 --> 03:02:45,130
 So if I go into here, into Y-frame, I need to create a face on that.

3302
03:02:45,130 --> 03:02:47,010
 And I can do that by hitting F, okay?

3303
03:02:47,010 --> 03:02:50,490
 So I've now got a face and it's completely flat across it.

3304
03:02:50,490 --> 03:02:51,830
 And this is okay.

3305
03:02:51,830 --> 03:02:54,170
 And this would, you know, it would work for most things.

3306
03:02:54,170 --> 03:02:57,330
 But there is a small detail that you notice when you look at like,

3307
03:02:57,330 --> 03:03:00,090
 like if you've got coffee on your desk right now,

3308
03:03:00,090 --> 03:03:05,950
 if you have a look at it, where the coffee, like where the water meets the actual edge there,

3309
03:03:05,990 --> 03:03:11,010
 there's like surface tension and it actually rises up against the edge there.

3310
03:03:11,010 --> 03:03:14,050
 And you might think that is like a negligible detail,

3311
03:03:14,050 --> 03:03:16,890
 but that line there always catches light.

3312
03:03:16,890 --> 03:03:20,170
 And when it's missing, the eye goes like, that looks fake.

3313
03:03:20,170 --> 03:03:24,490
 So it's a very small thing, but we can add that line in here very simply.

3314
03:03:24,490 --> 03:03:27,630
 So in edit mode, going back in Y-frame mode.

3315
03:03:27,630 --> 03:03:30,730
 So with our solid face here, I can create an inset.

3316
03:03:30,730 --> 03:03:31,250
 Okay.

3317
03:03:31,250 --> 03:03:32,150
 Have we talked about insets?

3318
03:03:32,150 --> 03:03:32,750
 I'm trying to think.

3319
03:03:32,750 --> 03:03:34,630
 Yeah, we did a little inset.

3320
03:03:34,630 --> 03:03:35,590
 Yeah, we've done some insets.

3321
03:03:35,590 --> 03:03:35,850
 Okay.

3322
03:03:35,990 --> 03:03:38,790
 But yeah, you hit I, okay, to do an inset.

3323
03:03:38,790 --> 03:03:44,030
 And that's just like creating a line like that goes all the way around that one single face there.

3324
03:03:44,030 --> 03:03:47,090
 And now I can put it like, yeah, let's go right there.

3325
03:03:47,090 --> 03:03:51,870
 And then right underneath it, I'm just going to, sorry, yeah, right here.

3326
03:03:52,110 --> 03:03:56,810
 I'm going to hit G and then Z to bring it down like so.

3327
03:03:56,810 --> 03:04:02,090
 And then now let's have a look at it in solid view mode and look at that.

3328
03:04:02,090 --> 03:04:04,410
 So I've now got it and it's pretty good.

3329
03:04:04,410 --> 03:04:08,830
 I might just do another inset because it's got this like crimped kind of effect there.

3330
03:04:08,830 --> 03:04:12,630
 So I'm going to hit I just so that it's not so noticeable on that portion.

3331
03:04:12,630 --> 03:04:14,730
 Okay, nice.

3332
03:04:14,730 --> 03:04:19,270
 And now all I need to do is right click it and say shade smooth.

3333
03:04:19,270 --> 03:04:21,950
 So now I've got a little raised edge to it.

3334
03:04:21,950 --> 03:04:23,390
 Excellent.

3335
03:04:24,090 --> 03:04:27,810
 By the way, quick little opportunity to talk about something.

3336
03:04:27,810 --> 03:04:30,450
 Like it's kind of hard to see that raised edge there.

3337
03:04:30,450 --> 03:04:35,030
 It is there, but it's just like the lighting doesn't really make it visible.

3338
03:04:35,030 --> 03:04:39,890
 So sometimes when you're modeling, it can be helpful to change the way things are displayed here.

3339
03:04:39,890 --> 03:04:42,650
 So there's a really powerful tool in the top right-hand corner.

3340
03:04:42,650 --> 03:04:44,930
 The default lighting is studio.

3341
03:04:44,930 --> 03:04:50,150
 If you go to matte cap and then click this ball here, you've got all these options,

3342
03:04:50,150 --> 03:04:52,410
 which can show you different views.

3343
03:04:52,930 --> 03:04:58,450
 It's actually how I create some of my 3D, my YouTube thumbnails because some of these look really beautiful.

3344
03:04:58,450 --> 03:05:05,910
 But you can see that's so much more useful for seeing what that edge actually looks like there on that.

3345
03:05:05,910 --> 03:05:11,750
 And there's some like this, which are designed to like show you where there is like artifacting on your mesh

3346
03:05:11,750 --> 03:05:16,610
 because it'll reveal problems with the mesh that you otherwise might not have seen.

3347
03:05:16,610 --> 03:05:19,330
 And these are new in Blender 5.0.

3348
03:05:19,330 --> 03:05:21,750
 They redid the matte caps, I should say.

3349
03:05:22,170 --> 03:05:23,550
 But anyways, it's cool.

3350
03:05:23,550 --> 03:05:25,910
 It's nice to have, but we don't really need it.

3351
03:05:25,910 --> 03:05:26,530
 Okay.

3352
03:05:26,530 --> 03:05:30,770
 So to give it a coffee foam texture, I'm going to hit N to bring up my properties over here.

3353
03:05:30,770 --> 03:05:34,470
 And then I'm going to download my coffee foam atlas texture.

3354
03:05:34,470 --> 03:05:35,550
 So let's hit download.

3355
03:05:35,550 --> 03:05:37,530
 And then with it selected, I'm going to hit apply.

3356
03:05:37,530 --> 03:05:41,270
 And if I go into rendered view mode, it should look like that.

3357
03:05:42,690 --> 03:05:45,530
 Let's go to the UV editor.

3358
03:05:45,530 --> 03:05:46,230
 Okay.

3359
03:05:46,230 --> 03:05:47,670
 Because I don't think it's been UV unwrapped.

3360
03:05:47,670 --> 03:05:49,270
 Nope.

3361
