03:22:37,870 --> 03:22:38,670
 So, how do we do that?

3672
03:22:38,670 --> 03:22:42,230
 Well, there is a way to reference an attribute inside of this.

3673
03:22:42,230 --> 03:22:42,870
 I'll show you soon.

3674
03:22:42,870 --> 03:22:46,290
 But the attribute we're going to use is called weight painting.

3675
03:22:46,290 --> 03:22:53,730
 And you can activate that by going at the top of your screen there and changing from object mode to weight paint mode.

3676
03:22:53,730 --> 03:22:58,130
 And I'm going to deactivate my sprinkles so that I can just see the icing.

3677
03:22:58,130 --> 03:23:03,430
 Now, if you were to click and drag across it when it is active in this state, you'll see that you get like a heat map.

3678
03:23:03,590 --> 03:23:07,290
 And as you can probably guess, where it is red is going to be where your sprinkles are.

3679
03:23:07,290 --> 03:23:09,870
 And where it is black, it's where your sprinkles are not going to be.

3680
03:23:09,870 --> 03:23:15,850
 And if you want to get technical, weight paint mode is actually adding an attribute to the mesh, which is where it is

3681
03:23:15,850 --> 03:23:17,570
 red, that is a value of one.

3682
03:23:17,570 --> 03:23:19,990
 Where it is black is a value of zero.

3683
03:23:20,230 --> 03:23:25,930
 And then varying values between there, essentially, a heat map of values, which are stored in the mesh.

3684
03:23:25,930 --> 03:23:29,090
 And then other parts of Blender can reference this.

3685
03:23:29,090 --> 03:23:34,510
 So, it's a very quick way to just do some painting on a mesh to reference in different things.

3686
03:23:34,510 --> 03:23:41,580
 Now, what is not clear, though, is that when you're painting in this mode, if you go into the isolated view by hitting

3687
